window.AGRO_HANDBOOK_SEARCH = [{"title":"Agro Core and AgroVision","group":"Project overview","page":"project-overview.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-readme-md--agro-core-and-agrovision","text":"Agro Core is a farm-scoped agricultural operations API, and AgroVision is its Spanish-language web interface. Together they cover farm setup, field work, crop cycles, irrigation, nutrient management, inspections, production records, and agricultural maps. This repository contains an MVP with a local demonstration mode and a server-backed mode. Demo data is visibly labeled and isolated from real farm records. The system records agricultural workflows and technical reviews; it does not claim to automate agronomic diagnosis, prescriptions, or decisions."},{"title":"Project handbook","anchor":"doc-readme-md--project-handbook","text":"Project handbook Open the Agro Core project handbook for a searchable, chapter-based view of the project overview, architecture diagrams, requirements, implementation details, agronomic and module guides, frontend workflows, and operations notes. Every guide has its own page and table of contents, and includes the full contents of the maintained Markdown guide. After updating those guides, refresh the handbook with python3 scripts/generate project handbook.py from the repository root."},{"title":"Module guides","anchor":"doc-readme-md--module-guides","text":"Module guides - Fertilización: análisis de laboratorio, interpretación preliminar revisable, perfiles, catálogo de productos, planes aprobados únicos o recurrentes, tareas por ocurrencia, aplicaciones parciales con fotos y seguimiento técnico. Proceso y límites. - Water management: irrigation sectors, moisture monitoring, schedules, execution, water usage, sources, quality, infrastructure, balances, maintenance, and sensor checks. Implementation details."},{"title":"Current implementation snapshot","anchor":"doc-readme-md--current-implementation-snapshot","text":"Current implementation snapshot Area Available now Important boundary --- --- --- Farm access Registration, session login, farm creation, per-farm ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, AGRONOMIST, and WORKER roles. Password recovery, email verification, and membership removal are not implemented. Farm structure Farm → field → block → row, with SRID 4326 polygons and row lines. Measurements are supplied or estimated values; there are no update endpoints for fields, blocks, or rows. Work tasks Persistent task assignment and status flow for field/block work, blockers, notes, review, and approval. No row targeting, rescheduling, structured execution quantities, task evidence, or offline synchronization. Planting Catalogs, crop cycles, site assessments, planting-material lots, approved plans, execution, establishment checks, stage history, and regional calendars. Calendar/stage automation, complete catalog lifecycle, and cycle audit history remain open gaps. Irrigation Moisture records, thresholds, one-time plans, daily/weekly recurring schedules, execution, water records, and sensor checks. No automatic valves, weather optimization, external telemetry gateway, or complete hydraulic map. Fertilization Soil/foliar analysis, configurable profiles, provisional interpretation, agronomist review, product catalogs, approved recurring/one-time plans, applications, and follow-up. No fertilizer prescription, inventory, cost management, or lab integration. Inspections and prevention Managed pest/disease catalog, inspections, review, follow-ups, treatments, manual preventive assignments, and evidence. Records are not a verified diagnosis; risk-triggered treatment workflows are not implemented. Harvest Structured, editable HARVEST records linked to field/block/row and crop cycle, with quantities, units, quality, rejection, and variance. Harvest planning and bunch-level traceability remain open gaps. Sensors and analytics Sensor/readings APIs, configurable threshold alerts, dashboard and map summaries, and water-use analytics. No physical IoT gateway or predictive recommendations. Photos Authenticated JPEG/PNG upload, view, and download for events and inspections/treatments. Photo bytes are stored in PostgreSQL; S3 uploads and presigned URLs are not implemented. See system requirements for the full FR-01–FR-29 and NFR-01–NFR-16 status matrix, and the module documents below for workflow details and evidence."},{"title":"AgroVision frontend","anchor":"doc-readme-md--agrovision-frontend","text":"AgroVision frontend The React / TypeScript / Vite frontend includes responsive desktop and mobile layouts, a sidebar, global view search, farm selection, and these screens: - Dashboard with farm metrics, recent activity, interactive map, and charts. - Daily work with filtering, farm-member assignment, field/block targeting, creation, execution, blocking, review, and approval persisted in PostgreSQL. - Farm maps with interactive fields, blocks, and rows, zoom, filters, layer controls, and health, moisture, nutrient, and disease-observation views. Demo layers are illustrative. Real mode draws stored field/block polygons and row lines over OpenStreetMap, with Esri World Imagery satellite and hybrid options. - Fields, blocks, and rows with creation forms, parent filters, and confirmed deletion according to the current user's permissions. - Planting cycles, site/material approvals, planting execution, establishment, and regional planting calendars. - Irrigation sectors, moisture readings, recurring scheduling, execution, water administration, and usage reports. - Soil/foliar analyses, nutrient profiles, approved fertilizer plans, and application follow-up. - Pest/disease inspections, preventive assignments, treatments, and event history. - Structured crop-cycle harvest records alongside legacy harvest events. - Event creation and detail, authenticated photo uploads, legacy media references, filtered CSV export, and authorized deletion. - Agronomic center, account details, farm information, and team role assignment. - Registration and login with session cookies and automatic CSRF handling."},{"title":"Nutrient management coverage","anchor":"doc-readme-md--nutrient-management-coverage","text":"Nutrient management coverage The current fertilization workflow covers the three core nutrient-management requirements at MVP level: Capability Current coverage Status --- --- --- Track soil nutrient levels Soil and foliar analyses record N, P, K, pH, organic matter, Ca, Mg, S, B, and Zn with units, methods, laboratory, date, block, reference, and report evidence. Implemented Identify deficiencies Compatible banana/plantain soil results receive a provisional Low, Adequate, High, or Unknown interpretation with the matched range and explanation. Partial: technical review remains required Monitor fertilizer applications Approved plans and application records support catalog products, doses, units, quantities, dates, recurrence, occurrence tasks, target coverage, lots, evidence, partial applications, totals, and follow-up. Implemented Interpretations are preliminary and do not generate fertilizer recommendations, doses, or prescriptions. Configurable farm/crop/variety profiles and persisted agronomist review are implemented; method-aware validation, confidence scoring, and inventory/cost controls remain open gaps. See fertilization workflow for the operational limits, API endpoints, and frontend screens."},{"title":"Run the frontend","anchor":"doc-readme-md--run-the-frontend","text":"Run the frontend Requires Node.js 22.18+ and npm. From the repository root: cd frontend npm ci npm run dev Open http://127.0.0.1:5173/ harvest to open the structured Cosecha screen directly. The app starts in a visibly labeled demonstration that works without the Java API. Use Conectar mi finca to register or log in to the real backend. API requests are proxied from /api to http://localhost:8080; override the target with AGRO API URL when launching Vite if needed. Demo changes are stored in this browser under agrovision. local-storage keys. Real authentication uses HTTP-only server session cookies, not local-storage tokens. The real mode does not substitute demo tasks, sensors, yields, or AI results when the backend lacks those features. The aerial demo map is an AI-generated illustration, not satellite imagery of an actual farm. npm test npm run build npx playwright install chromium npm run test:e2e The production build is written to frontend/dist. Production hosting needs an /api reverse proxy to the backend and same-site session cookies. The Vite development proxy is not included in the production build. See frontend/README.md for screens, limitations, and testing."},{"title":"Implemented features","anchor":"doc-readme-md--implemented-features","text":"Implemented features"},{"title":"Farm, field, block, and row management","anchor":"doc-readme-md--farm-field-block-and-row-management","text":"Farm, field, block, and row management The location hierarchy is Farm → Field → Block → Row . Resource Operations Stored information List filters --- --- --- --- Farm Create, list, retrieve by ID, delete Name, country, region, creation timestamp Current user's farm memberships Field Create, list, retrieve by ID, delete Parent farm, name, polygon boundary, supplied area in hectares, creation timestamp farmId Block Create, list, retrieve by ID, delete Parent field, code, crop (defaults to banana), variety, polygon boundary, supplied area in hectares, creation timestamp fieldId Row Create, list, retrieve by ID, delete Parent block, row code, geographic line, supplied length in meters, plant spacing (defaults to 2 meters), estimated plant count, creation timestamp blockId Lists are restricted to farms the user belongs to. Block codes are unique within a field; row codes are unique within a block. Areas, lengths, and estimated plant counts are supplied by the client rather than calculated automatically. There are no update endpoints for these resources."},{"title":"Agricultural events and work history","anchor":"doc-readme-md--agricultural-events-and-work-history","text":"Agricultural events and work history - Create, search, retrieve by ID, and delete agricultural events. - Record occurrence time, notes, optional GPS location, and creation timestamp. - Link an event to a block, a row, or both. When both are supplied, the row must belong to that block. - Identify an affected plant range using plantIndexStart and plantIndexEnd; individual plants are not separate managed records. - Record the authenticated user as the event author. - Search using optional farmId, blockId, rowId, eventType, from, and to filters. Date bounds are inclusive and use timestamps such as 2026-10-16T00:00:00Z. Supported event types: Type Purpose --- --- SCOUTING OBSERVATION Record crop inspections and suspected pest/disease observations TREATMENT APPLICATION Record that a treatment was applied FERTILIZER APPLICATION Record that fertilization occurred IRRIGATION EVENT Record that watering/irrigation occurred HANDLING EVENT Record crop handling activities SOIL SAMPLE Record that a soil sample was taken HARVEST EVENT Record that harvesting occurred These event records are separate from scheduled work tasks and preserve the activity history. Structured crop monitoring data is stored through the monitoring-record API described below. Events default to status OPEN, with no status-transition endpoint."},{"title":"Scouting observations","anchor":"doc-readme-md--scouting-observations","text":"Scouting observations - Attach suspectedCatalogId, severity, and symptoms to a scouting event when an observation payload is provided. - Associate the observation with a block, row, or plant range through its event. - Store a suspected catalog reference; the dedicated inspection workflow also uses the managed phytosanitary catalog and only active problems can be used for new inspections. The database includes an ai diagnosis json column, but there is no implemented AI diagnosis service or API workflow. Observation details are persisted on event creation; there is no separate observation read/update endpoint."},{"title":"Disease map layer","anchor":"doc-readme-md--disease-map-layer","text":"Disease map layer - Return a GeoJSON FeatureCollection containing row LineString geometries. - Include rowId, rowCode, maximum severity, observation count, and latest observation timestamp for each returned row. - Filter by optional farmId, from, and to, within the user's farm access. - Include rows with associated scouting observation details; this is an observation-based summary, not a confirmed disease diagnosis or prediction. The backend supplies map data; it does not include a map viewer."},{"title":"Media records","anchor":"doc-readme-md--media-records","text":"Media records - Register media references against an existing event. - Store media kind, S3 object key, content type, capture timestamp, optional GPS location, and JSON metadata. - List media records by eventId. The legacy /media module stores references and metadata. Actual JPEG/PNG photos are uploaded, viewed, and downloaded through /photos, described below. S3 client integration and presigned URLs are not implemented. There are no media-reference update or delete endpoints."},{"title":"Fotos de eventos, inspecciones y tratamientos","anchor":"doc-readme-md--fotos-de-eventos-inspecciones-y-tratamientos","text":"Fotos de eventos, inspecciones y tratamientos Desde el detalle de un evento o inspección se pueden elegir fotos, usar la opción de cámara del dispositivo, ver miniaturas, ampliar y descargar las imágenes. En inspecciones se puede seleccionar un tratamiento como destino de la foto. Primero se guarda el registro y después se adjuntan las fotos desde su detalle. Las fotos reales se guardan como datos binarios en PostgreSQL mediante la migración V8; no requieren S3 ni un directorio compartido de archivos. Los respaldos de la base deben incluir photo asset. Las claves S3 anteriores siguen disponibles como referencias externas y no se convierten automáticamente en fotos cargadas. Endpoint Comportamiento --- --- POST /api/v1/photos Multipart con file y eventId o inspectionId; treatmentId opcional para inspecciones GET /api/v1/photos Lista metadatos según el mismo destino, sin devolver los bytes GET /api/v1/photos/{id}/content Imagen autenticada; ?download=true fuerza descarga La carga admite JPEG y PNG de hasta 10 MB y 24 megapíxeles; hasta 10 fotos por selección, enviadas individualmente. El navegador aplica la orientación de la imagen y la prepara para carga. El servidor verifica el contenido real y las dimensiones, decodifica y vuelve a codificar la imagen sin metadatos originales. Se conserva el nombre del archivo saneado, formato, tamaño, autor y fecha de carga. Cada carga, consulta y descarga exige sesión y pertenencia a la finca; las cargas usan CSRF. Las respuestas de imagen tienen no-store y nosniff. Una inspección anulada conserva sus fotos consultables, pero no admite más cargas. Al eliminar un evento se eliminan sus fotos por cascada. El modo demo guarda los blobs en IndexedDB, separados de los datos reales, conserva las fotos al recargar y las limpia al restablecer la demostración. Al eliminar eventos demo también se eliminan sus fotos. Las cargas fallidas muestran error y conservan las fotos que ya se guardaron en una selección múltiple."},{"title":"Authentication and authorization","anchor":"doc-readme-md--authentication-and-authorization","text":"Authentication and authorization - Register users and log in/out using HTTP sessions. - Retrieve the current user's profile and farm memberships. - Store BCrypt password hashes and normalized email addresses. - Protect state-changing requests with CSRF tokens; rotate session IDs and CSRF tokens at login. - Assign ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, AGRONOMIST, and WORKER roles independently for each farm. - Automatically give farm creators the administrator role on their new farm. - Let administrators add registered users to their farm or change their roles. - Restrict farm resources, events, media, and map results to farm members. See the permissions table and onboarding examples below for the complete flow."},{"title":"Geospatial storage and infrastructure","anchor":"doc-readme-md--geospatial-storage-and-infrastructure","text":"Geospatial storage and infrastructure - PostgreSQL/PostGIS storage with spatial indexes and SRID 4326 coordinates. - Point, polygon, and line input DTOs, with automatic closure of polygon rings; only the outer polygon ring is supported. - Spring Data JPA persistence, Hibernate Spatial, and JTS geometry conversion. - Flyway migrations evolve the schema from core farm/identity tables through planting, task, irrigation, fertilization, inspections, photos, and production workflows (currently through V24, with version gaps). - Basic request validation for required fields and authentication inputs. - Swagger/OpenAPI documentation and interactive API exploration. - Spring Boot Actuator health endpoint at /actuator/health. - Configurable browser CORS origins and session cookie settings. - Docker Compose database setup with persistent database storage. - Gradle wrapper and automated authentication, permission, membership, and application startup tests."},{"title":"Functional requirements","anchor":"doc-readme-md--functional-requirements","text":"Functional requirements Functional requirements describe what users can do and what the system must do in response . For example, a worker signs in, sees their assigned work, and records its completion. The statuses below distinguish current behavior from work that still needs development: - Implemented: available in the current backend. - In progress: the core workflow exists, but important capabilities remain incomplete. - Partial: some of the requirement is available, while one or more specified behaviors remain missing. - Missing: no usable end-to-end implementation exists yet. - Planned: missing work that is identified for a future delivery. For cross-product functional and non-functional requirements, status, and open gaps, see System Requirements. The detailed crop-management audit tracks its own workflow, endpoint inventory, and evidence. Module documentation is available for fertilization and water management."},{"title":"Accounts and farm access","anchor":"doc-readme-md--accounts-and-farm-access","text":"Accounts and farm access ID Requirement and expected behavior Status --- --- --- FR-01 A user can register with a name, unique normalized email, and valid password. Registration grants no access to existing farms. Implemented FR-02 A registered user can log in, retrieve their profile and farm roles, and log out. Invalid credentials are rejected; logout invalidates the session. Implemented FR-03 An authenticated user can create a farm and automatically become its administrator. Implemented FR-04 A farm administrator can assign an existing registered user to their farm as ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, AGRONOMIST, or WORKER and change that role. Changes apply to existing sessions; self-demotion is rejected. Implemented FR-05 A user can read only farm resources allowed by their memberships and perform only operations allowed by their farm role. Partial: farm scoping is enforced across the current API, but role-specific administrative permissions are not yet uniform across every module. FR-06 A user can recover a forgotten password and verify their email; an administrator can remove a farm membership. Planned"},{"title":"Agricultural records and location","anchor":"doc-readme-md--agricultural-records-and-location","text":"Agricultural records and location ID Requirement and expected behavior Status --- --- --- FR-07 Authorized users can create, list, retrieve, and delete farms, fields, blocks, and rows according to the permissions table. Child-resource lists support filtering by parent. Implemented FR-08 Users can provide field/block polygon boundaries, row lines, and supplied measurements such as area, length, spacing, and estimated plant count. Coordinates use SRID 4326. Implemented FR-09 Farm members can record the seven supported agricultural event types against a block or row, with occurrence time, notes, optional GPS, and an optional plant range. Authenticated identity determines authorship. Implemented FR-10 Farm members can search events by farm, block, row, type, and date range, retrieve an event, and let authorized managers delete events. Implemented FR-11 A scouting event can persist suspected catalog ID, severity, and symptoms. Dedicated inspections expose occurrence, affected plants/area, symptoms, review, follow-up, and managed pest/disease catalog selection. Partial: legacy event editing and richer catalog attributes remain FR-12 Farm members can request a GeoJSON disease observation layer with row geometry, maximum severity, observation count, and latest observation time, optionally filtered by farm/date. Implemented FR-13 Farm members can upload, view, and download authorized JPEG/PNG photos for events, inspections, and treatments, and retain legacy media references. Other file types and S3 integration remain outside the photo workflow. Implemented for photos"},{"title":"Daily work and agronomic planning","anchor":"doc-readme-md--daily-work-and-agronomic-planning","text":"Daily work and agronomic planning ID Requirement and expected behavior Status --- --- --- FR-14 An authorized manager can create a task with activity type, field or block, planned date/time, instructions, priority, and assigned worker. Partial: server-backed tasks support field/block targets; row targeting, editing, and rescheduling are pending. ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, and AGRONOMIST can manage tasks. FR-15 A manager can assign work to members of the same farm as the task. Cross-farm assignments are rejected. Implemented for individual farm members; crews are not supported. FR-16 A worker can open “My work today” to see their assigned tasks for the farm's local date and their outstanding overdue tasks, grouped or filtered by field/block. Partial: server-backed lists, filters and search implemented; device calendar used, farm timezone pending FR-17 A worker can start a task, report a blocker with a reason, and submit work for review with actual quantities, units, notes, time, and evidence as applicable. Partial: persisted transitions and notes implemented; quantities, execution time and evidence pending FR-18 An authorized manager can approve submitted work or return it with a reason. Approved execution is linked to agricultural history without duplicate completion records. Partial: ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, and AGRONOMIST can complete or return reviewed tasks; a return reason is not mandatory and task completion does not create/link an agricultural event. FR-19 Authorized managers can reschedule or cancel tasks and inspect task history, assignment, status changes, and completion evidence. Partial: managers can inspect current task assignment/status and update task status/notes; rescheduling, cancellation, reassignment, transition history, and completion evidence are unavailable. FR-20 An agronomist or authorized manager can configure crop/variety catalogs and production cycles by block, including growth stages and planned/actual planting and harvest dates. In progress: core farm-scoped catalogs, cycle records, workflow controls, and AGRONOMIST approvals implemented; richer catalog lifecycle, automatic stages, and reconciliation remain FR-21 Managers can configure planting windows by region, crop, and variety, and plan preparation, planting, and replanting. Perennial crops support continuous production without an annual-season requirement. In progress: regional rules, suggestions, perennial support, and cycle-window validation implemented; reusable templates, weather context, and automatic task generation remain FR-22 Approved fertilization plans specify products, doses, units, quantities, dates or recurrence, and target areas; workers record actual applications. Implemented for farm-scoped product catalog, approved plans, recurring occurrence tasks, task-linked applications, and evidence; inventory and cost controls remain pending FR-23 Pest/disease management connects observations, follow-up inspections, intervention criteria, and approved treatments to affected areas. Partial: structured observations with affected area, managed catalog, follow-ups, and manual preventive assignments implemented; risk criteria and treatment approval workflow pending FR-24 Irrigation plans specify target sectors, dates, and duration or volume; execution records actual watering. Water management records sources, quality, infrastructure, balances, maintenance, and sensor checks. Soil moisture is recorded manually or through sensors with configurable thresholds and alerts. Implemented for sectors, one-time and recurring plans, idempotent task generation, pause/resume/cancel, execution, water usage, moisture monitoring, alerts, sources, quality, infrastructure, balances, maintenance, sensor checks, and versioned management edits; permissions and complete hydraulic mapping remain pending FR-25 Soil sample records include structured laboratory results and can inform approved agronomic plans. Partial: nutrient levels, configurable farm/crop/variety profiles, provisional statuses, persisted agronomist review, and plan linkage are implemented; laboratory integration, method-aware confidence, and recommendations remain pending FR-26 Harvest plans identify target blocks, expected windows and instructions; execution records harvested quantities and quality. In progress: structured harvest capture by field/block/row and crop cycle is implemented; harvest planning remains pending FR-27 The system generates tasks from approved plans, dates, recurrence, and crop stages, and flags observations for review. Repeated generation must not duplicate tasks. Partial: fertilizer and irrigation recurrence generation is idempotent; planting tasks can be generated manually from approved plans. Calendar/stage-driven crop work and observation alerts remain planned. FR-28 Workers and supervisors receive assignment, overdue-work, and blocker notifications relevant to their responsibilities. Planned for task notifications; sensor threshold alerts and acknowledgment are separate implemented features. FR-29 A worker can capture work without connectivity and synchronize it later, with a visible synchronization state and conflict handling. Planned FR-14 through FR-19 now have a server-backed manual task workflow for field and block work. Remaining gaps include row targeting, structured execution data, evidence, task history, rescheduling, cancellation, and notifications. Automatic crop planning depends on approved agronomic data; generic event types alone do not supply doses, planting calendars, or treatment decisions. See the detailed system requirements matrix."},{"title":"Non-functional requirements","anchor":"doc-readme-md--non-functional-requirements","text":"Non-functional requirements Non-functional requirements describe how well the system must operate and the constraints it must respect , such as security, response time, reliability, and usability. A working endpoint does not by itself demonstrate that these qualities meet production expectations. In this section, Partial means some controls exist but the full requirement has not been implemented or verified. Proposed target means a planning value that needs agreement and measurement, not a current service guarantee. ID Quality requirement Current evidence and remaining work Status --- --- --- --- NFR-01 Protect credentials and authenticated sessions. BCrypt hashes, HTTP-only cookies, CSRF checks, login session/token rotation, and an eight-hour idle timeout exist. Production requires HTTPS with secure cookies; login abuse protection and account recovery are not implemented. Partial NFR-02 Isolate data and permissions by farm on every operation. Membership and role checks are used across current APIs and cross-farm cases have automated coverage. Some domain-specific management permission policies remain incomplete. Partial NFR-03 Preserve data integrity and atomic writes. Foreign keys, unique constraints, and transactions protect selected operations, including farm/owner creation and event/observation creation. Broader validation of plant ranges, measurements, severity, and geometry validity still needs definition and tests. Partial NFR-04 Keep common worker operations responsive under expected load. No load benchmark exists. Proposed target: p95 response time at most 2 seconds for login, daily-task listing, and task submission with 50 concurrent active users on a documented deployment and representative dataset, excluding file transfer. Proposed target NFR-05 Bound resource usage as agricultural history grows. Spatial indexes and selected relation/time indexes exist. Collection endpoints lack pagination; event/map reads can issue repeated lookups. Add bounded queries and benchmark growing datasets before claiming scalability. Partial NFR-06 Provide predictable service availability. A health endpoint exists, but uptime is not measured and no highly available deployment is configured. Proposed production target: 99.5% monthly availability, with measurement and maintenance rules agreed before rollout. Proposed target NFR-07 Recover agricultural data after infrastructure failure. Docker storage persists across container recreation, but it is not a backup. Scheduled backups and restore exercises are not configured. Proposed initial targets: at most 24 hours of data loss (RPO) and restoration within 4 hours (RTO). Partial NFR-08 Preserve or clearly recover worker sessions during deployment. Sessions are currently stored in one API process and disappear on restart. Multi-instance deployment needs shared sessions or a deliberate session strategy and user-visible reauthentication behavior. Partial NFR-09 Avoid duplicate or lost work during retries and concurrent updates. Task and domain transitions use optimistic versions; fertilizer and irrigation recurrence generation is idempotent. General request idempotency and offline synchronization remain pending. Partial NFR-10 Record accountable operational history. Events and workflow records retain authors and timestamps; selected decisions are versioned. There is no unified immutable audit log for login, membership, edits, deletions, and all workflow transitions. Partial NFR-11 Make failures diagnosable and API errors predictable. Health reporting, basic logging, and authentication/access status codes exist. Add consistent error bodies, correlation IDs, metrics, and alerting while keeping credentials and sensitive payloads out of logs. Partial NFR-12 Support maintainable, testable changes. Domain packages, Flyway migrations, the Gradle wrapper, OpenAPI documentation, and automated tests exist. Add CI checks and meaningful tests alongside new workflows. Partial NFR-13 Provide understandable, accessible field workflows. The frontend provides responsive Spanish layouts, labeled controls, keyboard-accessible dialogs, and visible task/block/status information. Browser tests cover desktop and mobile flows; evaluation with workers and a full accessibility audit remain pending. Partial NFR-14 Handle agricultural dates and units consistently. Events use timestamps and tasks use explicit dates/times; geometry uses SRID 4326 and many measurements name their units. Farm time zones and broader quantity/unit-pair validation remain missing. Partial NFR-15 Deploy reproducibly with environment-specific configuration. Database Compose setup, Flyway migrations, and configurable session/CORS settings exist. Production packaging/deployment, secret management, and production configuration need completion. Partial NFR-16 Minimize and protect personal and operational data. Farm authorization and access-controlled PostgreSQL photo storage exist. Data retention, account deletion/export policy, backup access, and formal data lifecycle controls remain undefined. Partial Performance, availability, and recovery targets must be confirmed against the expected farm count, worker count, data volume, connectivity, deployment budget, and business needs. No performance, uptime, or recovery certification is implied by the current automated tests."},{"title":"System diagrams","anchor":"doc-readme-md--system-diagrams","text":"System diagrams These diagrams preserve a useful view of the core identity, farm, geometry, and event model. They are conceptual and do not show every domain table or relationship. The current Flyway schema spans migrations V1 through V24 (with gaps), including tasks, planting, irrigation, fertilization, inspections, photos, and production. The architecture and component diagrams show the current single API service at a high level. The diagrams use Mermaid; open this file in a Markdown viewer with Mermaid support to render them."},{"title":"Entity relationship diagram","anchor":"doc-readme-md--entity-relationship-diagram","text":"Entity relationship diagram erDiagram app user --o{ farm membership : has farm --o{ farm membership : grants access farm --o{ field : contains field --o{ block : contains block --o{ row line : contains block o --o{ field event : targets row line o --o{ field event : targets field event --o observation detail : has field event --o{ media asset : has app user { bigint id PK text name text email UK text password hash timestamptz created at } farm membership { bigint farm id PK, FK bigint user id PK, FK varchar role \"ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, AGRONOMIST, WORKER\" } farm { bigint id PK text name text country text region timestamptz created at } field { bigint id PK bigint farm id FK text name geometry geom \"Polygon, SRID 4326\" double area ha timestamptz created at } block { bigint id PK bigint field id FK text code text crop text variety geometry geom \"Polygon, SRID 4326\" double area ha timestamptz created at } row line { bigint id PK bigint block id FK text row code geometry geom \"LineString, SRID 4326\" double length m double plant spacing m int plants estimated timestamptz created at } field event { bigint id PK event type event type timestamptz occurred at bigint created by user id \"Application-set author; no foreign key\" bigint block id FK \"Nullable\" bigint row id FK \"Nullable\" int plant index start int plant index end geometry geom \"Point, SRID 4326\" text notes text status timestamptz created at } observation detail { bigint event id PK, FK bigint suspected catalog id \"Legacy reference; not linked to current inspection catalog\" int severity text symptoms jsonb ai diagnosis json \"Storage only; no AI workflow\" } media asset { bigint id PK bigint event id FK text kind text s3 key text content type timestamptz captured at geometry gps geom \"Point, SRID 4326\" jsonb meta } farm membership has a composite primary key (farm id, user id). Additional unique constraints are (field id, code) on blocks and (block id, row code) on rows. An event must reference at least a block or a row; the API requires a matching block when both are supplied. created by user id` is populated from login but has no database foreign key, so historical author IDs remain intact."},{"title":"Sequence diagram — login and farm access","anchor":"doc-readme-md--sequence-diagram-login-and-farm-access","text":"Sequence diagram — login and farm access This example starts with an existing registered user. A worker needs an assigned farm membership before farm data appears in their list. sequenceDiagram actor User participant Client as API client participant Security as Spring Security participant Auth as AuthController participant Users as UserService participant Session as HTTP session store participant Farms as FarmController participant Access as FarmAccess participant Repo as FarmRepository participant DB as PostgreSQL / PostGIS User->>Client: Enter email and password Client->>Security: GET /api/v1/auth/csrf Security->>Auth: Provide CSRF token Auth-->>Client: 200 token + session cookie Client->>Security: POST /api/v1/auth/login + cookie + CSRF token Security->>Security: Validate CSRF token Security->>Auth: Validated login request Auth->>Users: Authenticate credentials Users->>DB: Find password hash by normalized email DB-->>Users: Hash or no matching user Users->>Users: Check BCrypt password alt Invalid credentials Users-->>Auth: Authentication failed Auth-->>Client: 401 Unauthorized else Valid credentials Users-->>Auth: User profile Auth->>Session: Rotate session ID, clear old CSRF token, save security context Auth->>Users: Load farm memberships Users->>DB: Join farm membership and farm DB-->>Users: Farms and roles Users-->>Auth: Profile and memberships Auth-->>Client: 200 profile + rotated session cookie Client->>Security: GET /api/v1/auth/csrf with updated cookie Security->>Auth: Provide new CSRF token Auth-->>Client: 200 new token for subsequent writes Client->>Security: GET /api/v1/farms with session cookie Security->>Session: Read authenticated security context Session-->>Security: Logged-in user Security->>Farms: Authenticated request Farms->>Access: Find current user's farm IDs Access->>DB: Query farm membership DB-->>Access: Allowed farm IDs Access-->>Farms: Allowed farm IDs Farms->>Repo: findAllById(allowed farm IDs) Repo->>DB: Fetch permitted farms DB-->>Repo: Farm records Repo-->>Farms: Farms Farms-->>Client: 200 permitted farms, or empty list Client-->>User: Display accessible farms Client->>Security: POST /api/v1/auth/logout + cookie + new CSRF token Security->>Session: Invalidate session and clear authentication Security-->>Client: 204 No Content end ``` Requests without authentication are rejected with 401 on protected endpoints; missing or invalid CSRF tokens on writes return 403. Access to a farm outside the user's membership, or an operation outside their role, returns 403."},{"title":"Architecture diagram","anchor":"doc-readme-md--architecture-diagram","text":"Architecture diagram The application is a single Spring Boot service with domain modules for identity, farm structure, tasks, planting, events, inspections/prevention, monitoring, irrigation, fertilization, telemetry, analytics, media, and photos. PostgreSQL stores agricultural and identity data; HTTP authentication sessions remain in application memory. flowchart TB Client[\"API clients: AgroVision React frontend, curl, Swagger UI\"] subgraph API[\"Agro Core — Spring Boot / Java 17\"] Security[\"Spring Security: session authentication, CSRF, CORS\"] Controllers[\"REST controllers: auth, farms, tasks, events, planting, inspections, irrigation, fertilization, monitoring, analytics, media\"] Services[\"Domain services: access, tasks, crop planning, water, nutrient, inspection, production, analytics\"] JPA[\"Spring Data JPA + Hibernate Spatial\"] JDBC[\"JdbcTemplate: users, memberships, access checks\"] Geometry[\"GeoMapper + JTS: point, polygon, line conversion\"] Sessions[(\"In-memory HTTP sessions\")] Flyway[\"Flyway schema migrations: V1–V24 (version gaps)\"] end subgraph Docker[\"Docker Compose database service\"] DB[(\"PostgreSQL 16 + PostGIS 3.4\")] Volume[(\"Persistent agro db volume\")] end Client --> \"HTTP / JSON; cookies and CSRF headers\" Security Security <--> Sessions Security --> Controllers Controllers --> Services Controllers --> JPA Controllers --> JDBC Services --> JPA Services --> JDBC Services --> Geometry Controllers --> Geometry JPA --> DB JDBC --> DB Flyway -.-> \"Schema migrations on startup\" DB DB --- Volume ``` The legacy media module stores S3 keys; photo uploads use the PostgreSQL-backed photo service. Swagger UI is served by the API; the AgroVision frontend runs separately through Vite or its static production build."},{"title":"Component diagram","anchor":"doc-readme-md--component-diagram","text":"Component diagram This component view uses a Mermaid flowchart to show the core identity, farm, event, geometry, and persistence dependencies. It is not an exhaustive map of the current domain packages. Some controllers read/write repositories directly; there is not a service layer between every controller and persistence. flowchart LR subgraph Configuration[\"Configuration\"] SecurityConfig[\"SecurityConfig + WebConfig\"] Encoder[\"BCrypt PasswordEncoder\"] end subgraph Identity[\"auth package\"] AuthController[\"AuthController\"] UserService[\"UserService\"] MembershipController[\"MembershipController\"] FarmAccess[\"FarmAccess\"] end subgraph Farm[\"farm package\"] FarmControllers[\"Farm, Field, Block, Row controllers\"] FarmServices[\"Farm, Field, Block, Row services\"] FarmRepos[\"Farm, Field, Block, Row repositories\"] end subgraph Events[\"events package\"] EventController[\"EventController\"] EventService[\"EventService\"] MapController[\"MapLayerController\"] EventRepos[\"FieldEvent + ObservationDetail repositories\"] end subgraph Media[\"media package\"] MediaController[\"MediaController\"] MediaRepo[\"MediaAssetRepository\"] end Geometry[\"geo package: GeoMapper + GeoDtos\"] JDBC[\"JdbcTemplate\"] JPA[\"Spring Data JPA / Hibernate Spatial\"] DB[(\"PostgreSQL / PostGIS\")] SecurityConfig --> Encoder AuthController --> UserService UserService --> Encoder UserService --> JDBC MembershipController --> UserService MembershipController --> FarmAccess MembershipController --> JDBC FarmAccess --> JDBC FarmControllers --> FarmAccess FarmControllers --> FarmServices FarmControllers --> FarmRepos FarmServices --> FarmRepos FarmServices --> Geometry FarmServices --> FarmAccess FarmServices --> JDBC EventController --> FarmAccess EventController --> EventService EventController --> EventRepos EventService --> FarmAccess EventService --> EventRepos EventService --> FarmRepos EventService --> Geometry MapController --> FarmAccess MapController --> EventRepos MapController --> FarmRepos MediaController --> FarmAccess MediaController --> MediaRepo MediaController --> EventRepos MediaController --> Geometry FarmRepos --> JPA EventRepos --> JPA MediaRepo --> JPA JPA --> DB JDBC --> DB Entities and DTOs belong to their respective domain packages. Farm creation also inserts the creator's administrator membership through JDBC in the same transaction as the JPA farm insert. FarmAccess` resolves resource ownership and checks farm roles against the database on each request that uses it."},{"title":"Prerequisites","anchor":"doc-readme-md--prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites - JDK 17. The Gradle toolchain requires Java 17 specifically; a newer JDK alone does not satisfy it. - Docker with Docker Compose for the local PostgreSQL/PostGIS database. - Node.js 22.18 or newer and npm to run the frontend. The Gradle wrapper downloads Gradle 8.14 and dependencies on first use, so the first build needs access to the configured artifact repositories."},{"title":"Start the database","anchor":"doc-readme-md--start-the-database","text":"Start the database docker compose up -d ```"},{"title":"Run the API","anchor":"doc-readme-md--run-the-api","text":"Run the API ./gradlew bootRun Service URL --- --- API http://localhost:8080 OpenAPI UI http://localhost:8080/swagger-ui/index.html OpenAPI document http://localhost:8080/v3/api-docs Health http://localhost:8080/actuator/health The Compose database uses the local development database agro with user and password agro on port 5432. Do not use these credentials in production. Override Spring settings with environment properties such as SPRING DATASOURCE URL, SPRING DATASOURCE USERNAME, SPRING DATASOURCE PASSWORD, and SERVER PORT. Set SESSION COOKIE SECURE=true` when serving over HTTPS."},{"title":"Main endpoints","anchor":"doc-readme-md--main-endpoints","text":"Main endpoints Resource Collection endpoint Supported methods Single-record endpoint --- --- --- --- Farms /api/v1/farms POST, GET GET, DELETE /api/v1/farms/{id} Fields /api/v1/fields POST, GET GET, DELETE /api/v1/fields/{id} Blocks /api/v1/blocks POST, GET GET, DELETE /api/v1/blocks/{id} Crop catalog /api/v1/catalog/crops?farmId={id} GET, POST POST /api/v1/catalog/crops/{cropId}/varieties Planting calendars /api/v1/planting-calendars?farmId={id} GET, POST GET /api/v1/planting-calendars/suggestions, PUT/DELETE /api/v1/planting-calendars/{id} Planting workflow /api/v1/planting?farmId={id} GET, POST All cycle, assessment, material, plan, execution, establishment, and stage routes are listed in the complete crop endpoint inventory Rows /api/v1/rows POST, GET GET, DELETE /api/v1/rows/{id} Tasks /api/v1/tasks?farmId={id} GET, POST PATCH /api/v1/tasks/{id} Events /api/v1/events POST, GET GET, DELETE /api/v1/events/{id} Monitoring and harvest records /api/v1/monitoring-records POST, GET GET, PATCH, DELETE /api/v1/monitoring-records/{id} Irrigation and water management /api/v1/irrigation GET, POST, PATCH Recurring-schedule actions, executions, and water-record routes; see water management Fertilization /api/v1/fertilization GET, POST Analysis, product/profile, plan, application, review, and follow-up routes; see fertilization Inspections and treatments /api/v1/inspections, /api/v1/phytosanitary-problems GET, POST, PATCH Review, cancellation, treatment, and evidence subresources Photos /api/v1/photos GET, multipart POST GET /api/v1/photos/{id}/content Sensor devices /api/v1/sensors POST, GET None Sensor readings /api/v1/sensors/{deviceId}/readings POST, GET None Alert rules /api/v1/alert-rules POST None Alerts /api/v1/alerts GET, POST acknowledge None Preventive measures /api/v1/preventive-measures?farmId={id} GET, POST DELETE /api/v1/preventive-measures/{id} Preventive assignments /api/v1/preventive-assignments?farmId={id} GET, POST, PATCH PATCH /api/v1/preventive-assignments/{id} Preventive evidence /api/v1/preventive-assignments/{id}/evidence GET, POST None Analytics map /api/v1/analytics/map GET None Analytics dashboard /api/v1/analytics/dashboard GET None Water-use analytics /api/v1/analytics/water-usage GET None Media /api/v1/media POST, GET (requires eventId) None Disease map /api/v1/map/rows/disease GET None Authentication and membership endpoints are listed in their own table below."},{"title":"Crop catalog","anchor":"doc-readme-md--crop-catalog","text":"Crop catalog Each farm has a reusable crop and variety catalog. Administrators, supervisors, and agronomists can add catalog entries from Configuración → Catálogo agrícola ; workers can select the approved entries when creating blocks, crop cycles, and planting material lots. Existing crop and variety text is retained for compatibility, while new records also store catalog identifiers. Existing farms are seeded with Banano / Cavendish and prior text values are migrated into catalog entries."},{"title":"Regional planting calendars","anchor":"doc-readme-md--regional-planting-calendars","text":"Regional planting calendars Each farm can configure recurring planting rules by region, crop, and optional variety from Configuración → Calendario regional . Annual rules store a month/day window and preparation lead time; perennial rules support continuous production without an annual season. The suggestions endpoint resolves the farm's region and prioritizes variety-specific rules over crop-wide rules. When a matching annual rule exists, cycle creation rejects a planting window outside the configured regional window; farms without rules remain compatible with manually planned cycles."},{"title":"Crop management status","anchor":"doc-readme-md--crop-management-status","text":"Crop management status This status covers crop management only : crop and variety catalogs, planting cycles, lote evaluation, planting plans, field execution, establishment, phenology, regional calendars, and agronomist access. Nutrient, water, pest/disease, and production management are separate modules and are not included in this estimate. Estimated coverage: approximately 80%. This is a functional estimate, not a test-coverage percentage: the core workflow is usable, while automation, richer catalog data, permissions, and auditability remain incomplete."},{"title":"Implemented","anchor":"doc-readme-md--implemented","text":"Implemented - Farm-scoped crop and variety records with catalog selection in planting workflows. - Crop-cycle creation by block with planting dates, area, expected harvest, and stage data. - Site/lote assessment with approval and rejection actions. - Planting-material lot registration with accept/reject decisions. - Planting-plan creation, approval, task creation, and field execution records. - Establishment checks for survival, mortality, replacement, and follow-up evidence. - Phenological stage observations and cycle stage history. - Regional annual/perennial planting rules, suggestions, and cycle-window validation."},{"title":"In progress","anchor":"doc-readme-md--in-progress","text":"In progress - Crop and variety catalog lifecycle, including richer agronomic attributes and edit/archive behavior. - Automatic cycle-stage progression, replant links, and planned-versus-actual cycle reconciliation. - Reusable calendar templates and stronger preparation, planting, and replanting workflows. - Agronomist role access and approvals; the current model is still role-wide."},{"title":"Missing","anchor":"doc-readme-md--missing","text":"Missing - Calendar- and growth-stage-driven automatic task generation with recurrence and duplicate protection. - Fine-grained agronomist permissions by capability, module, region, or time period. - A unified, immutable crop-management audit timeline across approvals and record changes."},{"title":"Planned next","anchor":"doc-readme-md--planned-next","text":"Planned next 1. Complete crop/variety catalog lifecycle and agronomic reference attributes. 2. Automate crop-stage transitions and calendar-driven preparation, planting, and replanting tasks. 3. Add delegated and time-bound agronomist permissions. 4. Add unified crop-cycle history, approvals, and change traceability."},{"title":"Geometry input format (simple GeoJSON-like DTO)","anchor":"doc-readme-md--geometry-input-format-simple-geojson-like-dto","text":"Geometry input format (simple GeoJSON-like DTO) - Point: { \"lng\": -74.1, \"lat\": 10.5 } - Polygon: { \"coordinates\": [[[lng,lat],[lng,lat],...,[lng,lat]]] } (outer ring only; server closes ring) - LineString: { \"coordinates\": [[lng,lat],[lng,lat],...] }"},{"title":"Crop monitoring records","anchor":"doc-readme-md--crop-monitoring-records","text":"Crop monitoring records /api/v1/monitoring-records stores structured records against a field, block, or row. Every record has a recordType, recordedAt, and location; non-harvest monitoring records still require a block. Supported types are CROP CYCLE, SOIL SAMPLE, FERTILIZER APPLICATION, IRRIGATION, HEALTH OBSERVATION, and HARVEST. The request supports crop and variety lifecycle fields; soil pH and N/P/K values; nutrient deficiencies; fertilizer product, dose, and quantity; irrigation duration, water volume, and soil moisture; pest or disease, severity, symptoms, prevention, treatment, follow-up, and resolution; and estimated/actual yield, quality grade, and rejected quantity, rejection reason, harvested area, and crop-cycle linkage. Harvest units are controlled (g, kg, t, lb, cajas, racimos, unidades) and quality grades are controlled (PREMIUM, A, B, C, REJECTED). Records can be filtered by farmId, blockId, fieldId, cropCycleId, recordType, from, and to. Farm membership is enforced for reads and writes, while deletion requires an agronomic role. The Cosecha y producción screen uses structured HARVEST records rather than the legacy generic HARVEST EVENT activity form. It captures the location hierarchy, cycle, explicit harvest date, area, estimate, actual quantity, unit, quality, rejected quantity, rejection reason, and notes, and shows per-unit totals plus estimated-versus- actual variance. Each record can be opened in a detail view or edited through PATCH /api/v1/monitoring-records/{id} with the same validation rules as creation. Existing generic harvest events remain available in the activity history for backward compatibility."},{"title":"Sensors, alerts, and analytics","anchor":"doc-readme-md--sensors-alerts-and-analytics","text":"Sensors, alerts, and analytics"},{"title":"Inspecciones fitosanitarias","anchor":"doc-readme-md--inspecciones-fitosanitarias","text":"Inspecciones fitosanitarias La pantalla Plagas y enfermedades permite registrar inspecciones por bloque y surco opcional. El catálogo inicial incluye Sigatoka negra, moko, marchitez por Fusarium, bacteriosis, picudos y nematodos; ahora puede administrarse desde Configuración → Catálogo fitosanitario . Se registran fecha, plantas inspeccionadas/afectadas, área afectada en hectáreas, síntomas, severidad porcentual con método de evaluación y próxima visita. La identidad autenticada determina el responsable. La incidencia se calcula como 100 × afectadas / inspeccionadas; es incidencia por plantas de la muestra, no por área. El área afectada se captura explícitamente en hectáreas y, cuando el bloque tiene área configurada, no puede superarla. No se infiere un diagnóstico ni un tratamiento. Todas las inspecciones se crean como sospechosas. Administradores y supervisores pueden confirmar, descartar o marcar un hallazgo resuelto dejando justificación, responsable y fecha de revisión. Las versiones evitan sobrescribir revisiones concurrentes. Los resultados confirmados deben sustentarse en la evaluación técnica correspondiente; este módulo no verifica resultados del ICA. Endpoint Operación --- --- GET /api/v1/phytosanitary-problems?farmId={id}[&includeInactive=true] Consultar catálogo activo o catálogo completo autorizado POST /api/v1/phytosanitary-problems Crear plaga o enfermedad; requiere acceso agronómico PATCH /api/v1/phytosanitary-problems/{code} Editar nombre/categoría o activar/desactivar; requiere acceso agronómico DELETE /api/v1/phytosanitary-problems/{code}?farmId={id} Desactivar sin borrar las inspecciones históricas POST /api/v1/inspections Crear inspección como miembro de la finca GET /api/v1/inspections Historial con filtros farmId, blockId, problemCode, from, to GET /api/v1/inspections/{id} Consultar detalle autorizado PATCH /api/v1/inspections/{id} Revisar: assessmentStatus, resolved, reviewNotes, version Migraciones V6 y V22: catálogo, área afectada e inspecciones. El modo demo usa registros locales separados del servidor y permite probar captura, revisión y persistencia. La capa de salud usa la última inspección por problema y destino (bloque/surco). Una evaluación se considera reciente hasta la próxima visita y como máximo por 30 días desde la inspección, con fechas de referencia UTC. Este límite operativo no es una frecuencia agronómica recomendada. Rojo significa hallazgos activos; verde, muestra reciente sin hallazgos activos; gris, falta de evaluación reciente. Las evaluaciones antiguas o resueltas no mantienen el bloque rojo. Un hallazgo activo vencido conserva el estado desconocido aunque exista una evaluación reciente de otro problema. El resumen no certifica sanidad de todo el bloque. Los eventos antiguos se conservan como historial y no colorean esta capa. Pruebas aisladas sin PostgreSQL: ./gradlew test --tests com.yourco.agro.InspectionApiTest. Prueba de navegador: cd frontend && npx playwright test tests/inspections.spec.ts."},{"title":"Medidas preventivas","anchor":"doc-readme-md--medidas-preventivas","text":"Medidas preventivas La pestaña Prevención convierte las buenas prácticas fitosanitarias en trabajo programado. Los administradores, supervisores y agrónomos pueden crear una biblioteca de medidas reutilizables con instrucciones; cualquier miembro puede asignar una medida a un bloque y responsable. La asignación conserva fecha, estado (PLANNED, IN PROGRESS o COMPLETED), resultado, autor y versión para evitar actualizaciones concurrentes. Una medida completada requiere notas de cierre y puede conservar referencias de imágenes, documentos o videos. Endpoint Operación --- --- GET, POST /api/v1/preventive-measures Consultar o crear medidas por finca DELETE /api/v1/preventive-measures/{id} Desactivar una medida; solo acceso agronómico GET, POST /api/v1/preventive-assignments Consultar o asignar medidas a bloques PATCH /api/v1/preventive-assignments/{id} Actualizar estado y resultado con version GET, POST /api/v1/preventive-assignments/{id}/evidence Consultar o adjuntar referencias de evidencia Migración V16: biblioteca, asignaciones y evidencia preventiva. El modo demo persiste el mismo flujo en el navegador. Las asignaciones manuales son la primera entrega; los disparadores por riesgo, recurrencia y generación automática de tareas siguen pendientes."},{"title":"Tratamientos, evidencias y conservación del historial","anchor":"doc-readme-md--tratamientos-evidencias-y-conservación-del-historial","text":"Tratamientos, evidencias y conservación del historial La pantalla conserva dos pestañas: Inspecciones e Historial anterior y tratamientos . La segunda recupera la consulta, captura de eventos, referencias de archivos, exportación CSV y eliminación autorizada del flujo anterior; no se migran ni eliminan los eventos existentes. Desde el detalle de una inspección se registran tratamientos realizados con fecha, descripción, producto opcional y cantidad/unidad opcionales. Las cantidades y unidades deben informarse juntas. Una evidencia puede referenciar la inspección o uno de sus tratamientos. Se conserva el autor autenticado. Estas evidencias son referencias S3 y metadatos. La sección Fotos adjuntas permite además cargar, ver y descargar fotos reales, independientemente de esas referencias. Los tratamientos nuevos se almacenan vinculados a la inspección, se consultan y exportan desde su detalle y no se duplican como eventos genéricos. El seguimiento crea una nueva inspección enlazada a su origen, con el mismo problema y ubicación. El detalle permite navegar entre el origen y sus seguimientos. Los gestores pueden anular , con motivo y versión vigente, en lugar de borrar una inspección. Se conservan fecha, autor, motivo, tratamientos y evidencias; la inspección anulada se excluye de la capa de salud y no admite más revisiones, tratamientos, evidencias ni seguimientos nuevos. Sus seguimientos existentes conservan su propia evaluación. La anulación no equivale a resolver un hallazgo. Endpoint Operación --- --- POST /api/v1/inspections/{id}/cancel Anular con reason y version; solo gestores GET, POST /api/v1/inspections/{id}/treatments Consultar o registrar tratamiento ejecutado GET, POST /api/v1/inspections/{id}/evidence Consultar o registrar referencia de archivo; treatmentId opcional POST /api/v1/inspections con parentInspectionId Registrar seguimiento vinculado Migración V7: anulación, vínculo de seguimiento, tratamientos y evidencias. La exportación de inspecciones respeta filtros y búsqueda, incluye anulaciones y protege las celdas frente a fórmulas de hojas de cálculo. El detalle permite exportar sus tratamientos, incluyendo referencias de evidencia asociadas. El modo demo persiste estos datos junto a las inspecciones locales y permite restablecerlos desde Configuración. Sensor devices belong to a farm and can optionally target a block. Devices ingest timestamped readings for soilMoisture, airHumidity, airTemperature, and soilTemperature through /api/v1/sensors/{deviceId}/readings. Readings are retained for history and can be polled by time range; the ingestion API is ready for an IoT gateway or MQTT/HTTP adapter. Managers can create alert rules using a metric, LT, LTE, GT, or GTE operator, threshold, severity, and message. Matching readings create open alerts that users can list by farm/status and acknowledge. This provides threshold-based moisture, temperature, and humidity alerts without making agronomic decisions automatically. /api/v1/analytics/map returns a GeoJSON block layer with the latest telemetry, condition status (DRY, OPTIMAL, WET, or NO DATA), harvest totals, and pest-risk severity. /api/v1/analytics/dashboard?farmId=1 returns block count, fertilizer quantity, water volume, harvested quantity, health observations, open alerts, and connected-device counts. /api/v1/analytics/water-usage returns date-scoped planned and actual irrigation volume by sector/block, measured versus estimated consumption, rainfall, balance requirements, deficits, and soil-moisture reading context for the Reports screen. Suitability and recommendation models remain domain rules to be calibrated with agronomists; current condition and risk scores are transparent threshold-based summaries."},{"title":"Users, login, and farm access","anchor":"doc-readme-md--users-login-and-farm-access","text":"Users, login, and farm access Authentication uses an HTTP session cookie (JSESSIONID), with CSRF protection. All agricultural endpoints require login and farm membership. Registration creates an account without farm access. Creating a farm gives its creator the ADMIN role for that farm. Roles are assigned independently per farm: Action ADMIN SUPERVISOR AGRONOMIST WORKER --- --- --- --- --- Read farm resources, events, map layers, and media Yes Yes Yes Yes Record events and register media Yes Yes Yes Yes Create/delete fields, blocks, and rows; delete events Yes Yes No No List farm members Yes Yes No No Assign/change member roles; delete farm Yes No No No Create and review farm tasks Yes Yes Yes No Update task status/notes Yes Yes Yes Yes, assigned tasks only Manage crop catalogs, regional calendars, and planting approvals Yes Yes Yes No Manage preventive-measure templates Yes Yes Yes No Assign, execute, and document preventive measures Yes Yes Yes Yes Any authenticated user can create a farm. Users can only list farms and resources they belong to. Farm administrators cannot demote themselves. Event authorship is taken from the logged-in user; a supplied createdByUserId is ignored."},{"title":"Authentication endpoints","anchor":"doc-readme-md--authentication-endpoints","text":"Authentication endpoints Method Endpoint Purpose --- --- --- GET /api/v1/auth/csrf Obtain CSRF token and session cookie POST /api/v1/auth/register Create account with name, email, password POST /api/v1/auth/login Login with email, password; return user and memberships GET /api/v1/auth/me Current user and farm memberships POST /api/v1/auth/logout Invalidate session; return 204 GET /api/v1/farms/{farmId}/members List farm members PUT /api/v1/farms/{farmId}/members Add an existing user or change their role using email, role Passwords must contain 12–64 characters and at most 72 UTF-8 bytes. They are stored as BCrypt hashes. Emails are normalized to lowercase. Duplicate registration returns 409; invalid credentials return 401; insufficient farm permissions return 403. Missing/invalid CSRF tokens return 403."},{"title":"Example onboarding","anchor":"doc-readme-md--example-onboarding","text":"Example onboarding Keep the cookies returned by the server and send the CSRF token in X-CSRF-TOKEN for every POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE request, including registration and login. curl -c /tmp/agro-cookies.txt http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/csrf Copy the response's token value into CSRF TOKEN, then register and login: CSRF TOKEN='paste-token-here' curl -b /tmp/agro-cookies.txt -c /tmp/agro-cookies.txt \\ -H \"X-CSRF-TOKEN: $CSRF TOKEN\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"name\":\"Farm Owner\",\"email\":\"owner@example.com\",\"password\":\"replace-this-password\"}' \\ http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/register curl -b /tmp/agro-cookies.txt -c /tmp/agro-cookies.txt \\ -H \"X-CSRF-TOKEN: $CSRF TOKEN\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"email\":\"owner@example.com\",\"password\":\"replace-this-password\"}' \\ http://localhost:8080/api/v1/auth/login Login rotates the session ID and CSRF token. Fetch /api/v1/auth/csrf again using the updated cookies and replace CSRF TOKEN with the new token before writing. Create a farm with POST /api/v1/farms and {\"name\":\"My Farm\"}. Have the worker register their own account, then assign them from the owner's session: curl -b /tmp/agro-cookies.txt -c /tmp/agro-cookies.txt \\ -X PUT -H \"X-CSRF-TOKEN: $CSRF TOKEN\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"email\":\"worker@example.com\",\"role\":\"WORKER\"}' \\ http://localhost:8080/api/v1/farms/1/members Replace 1 with the created farm ID. The worker's login response and /auth/me` will include that farm and role. Role changes take effect on existing sessions."},{"title":"Configuration and existing data","anchor":"doc-readme-md--configuration-and-existing-data","text":"Configuration and existing data - Sessions expire after eight hours of inactivity and are stored in server memory; restarting the API requires users to log in again. - Set SESSION COOKIE SECURE=true when serving the API over HTTPS in production. - Browser clients must send requests with credentials. CORS allows http://localhost:3000, http://localhost:5173, and http://127.0.0.1:5173 by default; configure APP CORS ALLOWED ORIGINS as a comma-separated list for other origins. - Cookies use SameSite=Lax. A frontend hosted on a different site requires a deployment-specific cookie configuration; using a same-site API proxy avoids this. - Migration V2 preserves existing agricultural data. Existing farms have no owner assigned automatically. A database operator must assign their initial administrator after that user registers, using the correct farm ID and email: INSERT INTO farm membership (farm id, user id, role) SELECT 1, id, 'ADMIN' FROM app user WHERE email = 'owner@example.com'; ```"},{"title":"Persistent work tasks","anchor":"doc-readme-md--persistent-work-tasks","text":"Persistent work tasks Migration V3 introduced tasks stored in PostgreSQL. Administrators, supervisors, and agronomists can create tasks assigned to a member of the same farm, targeting exactly one field or block. Workers list and update their assignments; agronomic managers list and review tasks in their farms. The UI includes selected-day and outstanding overdue work using the device calendar. Tasks are separate from agricultural events and from browser demo data. Endpoint Behavior --- --- GET /api/v1/tasks?farmId=1 List authorized tasks; farm filter optional POST /api/v1/tasks Create with farmId, fieldId or blockId, assigneeId, title, activity, date, time, priority, instructions PATCH /api/v1/tasks/{id} Update status, optional notes, and required current version; stale updates return 409 Activities: fertilization, irrigation, scouting, planting, harvest. Priorities: Alta, Media, Baja. States: pending → progress → review → completed. Pending/in-progress tasks can become blocked with a reason and resume as progress. ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, and AGRONOMIST can approve or return reviewed tasks to progress; a return reason is not currently required. Completing a task does not create an agricultural event. The PATCH endpoint changes task status and notes with an optimistic version; it does not edit assignment/details, reschedule, cancel, delete, attach evidence, or expose transition history."},{"title":"Remaining requirements and planned work","anchor":"doc-readme-md--remaining-requirements-and-planned-work","text":"Remaining requirements and planned work The core manual workflow Login → My work today → Instructions by field/block → Notes and execution → Manager review is implemented. The table summarizes known gaps; the complete requirement IDs and non-functional requirements remain in the system requirements matrix. Area Remaining work Current status --- --- --- Work tasks Row targeting; edit task details; reschedule, reassign, cancel; transition history; creation idempotency. Partial Assignments and crews Assign crews to work areas and tasks. Missing Daily work Farm time zone and richer task history. Partial; device calendar is used. Execution and review Capture structured quantities/time/evidence and link approval to agricultural history. Partial; task notes and state transitions exist. Crop catalog and production cycles Complete crop/variety lifecycle, automatic stage changes, reconciliation, and audit trail. In progress; see crop-management audit. Regional planting Reusable templates, weather context, and automatic preparation/replanting work. In progress. Perennial crops Continuous cycle planning and automated follow-up. Partial; perennial planting-calendar rules are available. Fertilization Inventory, cost controls, lab integration, method-aware confidence, and recommendations. Workflow implemented; these controls are missing. Pest and disease management Risk criteria, formal treatment approval, lab confirmation, and automatic interventions. Partial; structured inspections, follow-ups, and manual preventive work exist. Irrigation and water Complete hydraulic map, weather/meter integrations, broader administrative permissions, and offline work. Operational records and recurring generation implemented; these integrations/controls remain incomplete. Soil analysis Laboratory integration and validated, method-aware interpretation. Partial; profiles and provisional review are available. Harvest Harvest planning, bunch tracking, and full crop-cycle closeout. Partial; structured cycle-linked harvest capture is implemented. Automatic task generation Generate crop work from regional calendars, stages, and review flags. Missing; fertilizer and irrigation recurrence generation is implemented. Notifications Deliver assignment, overdue, and blocker notifications to workers/managers. Task notifications are planned; sensor threshold alerts and acknowledgment are available separately. Field connectivity Offline capture and later synchronization with conflict handling. Planned. Account lifecycle Password recovery, email verification, and membership removal. Planned. Doses and treatments must come from reviewed agronomic plans. Existing records do not calculate agronomic recommendations."},{"title":"Tests","anchor":"doc-readme-md--tests","text":"Tests Run the backend suite, frontend checks, and automated farm walkthrough with one command from the repository root. The browser tests open in Google Chrome, and the runner creates a disposable local QA database and farm. python3 scripts/run qa tests.py See the automated QA walkthrough for prerequisites, playback speed, and report locations. Authentication, CSRF, session rotation, and access checks can run without PostGIS: ./gradlew test --tests ' AuthIntegrationTest' The full suite also verifies application startup and farm membership assignment against the configured PostGIS database: ./gradlew test Run the Gradle commands with JDK 17 selected. The build uses a Java 17 toolchain; setting JAVA HOME` or the IDE Gradle JVM to a newer JDK does not provide that toolchain. The frontend checks are documented in the frontend README."}]},{"title":"Agro Core System Requirements","group":"Requirements and design","page":"system-requirements.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--agro-core-system-requirements","text":"Last reviewed: 2026-11-21 . This document tracks the cross-product functional and non-functional requirements for the Agro Core API and AgroVision. It describes the current behavior and gaps visible in the repository; a status of Implemented does not certify production readiness, performance, accessibility, or agronomic validity. Detailed module inventories are kept in the crop-management audit, fertilization guide, and water-management guide."},{"title":"Status definitions","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--status-definitions","text":"Status definitions Status Meaning --- --- Implemented The described capability exists in the current API and/or user workflow. Module documents identify any narrower limitations. Partial A useful part of the workflow exists, but one or more behaviors or controls in the requirement are missing. In progress Core workflow coverage exists and remaining development is listed with the requirement. Missing No usable implementation of the requirement exists. Planned Missing work has been identified for a future delivery; this is not a commitment or schedule. Proposed target A quality goal for discussion and measurement, not a current service guarantee."},{"title":"Functional requirements","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--functional-requirements","text":"Functional requirements"},{"title":"Accounts and farm access","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--accounts-and-farm-access","text":"Accounts and farm access ID Requirement Current state --- --- --- FR-01 Register with a name, unique normalized email, and valid password. A new account must not inherit access to existing farms. Implemented. Registration creates a user; farm membership is separate. FR-02 Sign in, read the current profile and farm memberships, and sign out. Reject invalid credentials and invalidate the session on logout. Implemented. Uses an HTTP session and CSRF-protected writes. FR-03 Let an authenticated user create a farm and grant the creator administrator access to it. Implemented. Farm and creator membership are created together. FR-04 Let a farm administrator add an existing user and assign or change that user's per-farm role. Implemented. Roles are ADMIN, SUPERVISOR, AGRONOMIST, and WORKER; self-demotion is rejected. Membership removal is not available. FR-05 Restrict farm data and actions by membership and role. Partial. Farm scoping is enforced across current domain APIs, but administrative permissions are not yet a complete, uniform policy across every module. See the module-specific permission gaps. FR-06 Support password recovery, email verification, and administrator removal of a farm membership. Planned. None of these account-lifecycle flows is available."},{"title":"Farm structure and agricultural history","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--farm-structure-and-agricultural-history","text":"Farm structure and agricultural history ID Requirement Current state --- --- --- FR-07 Create, list, retrieve, and delete farms, fields, blocks, and rows with parent filters and farm authorization. Implemented. Update operations are not exposed for these resources. FR-08 Store field/block polygons, row lines, and supplied measurements using longitude/latitude coordinates. Implemented. Geometry uses SRID 4326; polygon input currently supports one outer ring. Area, length, spacing, and plant counts are supplied or estimated values, not survey-derived measurements. FR-09 Record agricultural events against a block and/or row with type, occurrence time, notes, optional GPS, and optional plant range. The authenticated user determines authorship. Implemented. The seven event types are scouting, treatment, fertilizer, irrigation, handling, soil sample, and harvest. FR-10 Search and retrieve authorized events by farm, block, row, type, and time range; let authorized managers delete events. Implemented. Some events tied to approved irrigation or fertilization workflows cannot be deleted independently because their history must remain linked. FR-11 Record and review pest/disease observations, affected plants/area, symptoms, severity, follow-up, and managed problem selection. Partial. The dedicated inspection workflow supports catalog selection, review, follow-ups, treatments, and cancellation. Legacy event observations are not independently editable, and there is no laboratory confirmation or risk-triggered intervention workflow. FR-12 Provide an authorized GeoJSON disease-observation layer with row geometry, severity summary, count, and latest observation time. Implemented. This summarizes recorded observations; it is not a diagnosis or prediction. FR-13 Upload, view, and download authorized JPEG/PNG photos for events and inspections/treatments, retaining legacy media references. Implemented for photos. Photo bytes are stored in PostgreSQL. S3 uploads, presigned URLs, and other photo file types are not part of this workflow."},{"title":"Daily work and crop operations","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--daily-work-and-crop-operations","text":"Daily work and crop operations ID Requirement Current state --- --- --- FR-14 Let an authorized farm manager create and assign a dated task with activity, field/block, instructions, and priority. Partial. Admins, supervisors, and agronomists can assign farm members and target a field or block. Row targeting, rescheduling, and task editing are not available. FR-15 Assign a task only to a member of the same farm and prevent cross-farm assignments. Implemented. Assignment to crews is not available. FR-16 Let workers see their assigned work for a selected day and outstanding overdue tasks, with useful location filters. Partial. The UI supports task lists, date selection, search, and location filtering. It uses the device calendar because farms have no configured time zone. FR-17 Let workers start work, record blockers, and submit execution details for review. Partial. Status changes, blocker reasons, and notes are persisted. Actual quantities, execution time, and task evidence are not captured as structured task data. FR-18 Let an authorized manager approve or return submitted work and link approved execution to agricultural history without duplicate completion records. Partial. Admins, supervisors, and agronomists can complete or return a task from review; there is no required return reason or link from task completion to an agricultural event. FR-19 Let managers reschedule/cancel tasks and inspect assignment and status-transition history. Partial. Managers can list tasks and inspect their current assignment/status; task updates support status and notes with version checks. Rescheduling, cancellation, reassignment, and transition history are unavailable. FR-20 Configure crop/variety catalogs, production cycles, site assessments, planting material, plans, field execution, establishment, and stages. In progress. The first-phase workflow is available, including approvals and manual task generation. Catalog lifecycle, stage automation, cycle reconciliation, and a unified audit trail remain incomplete. See the crop-management audit. FR-21 Configure regional planting windows and use them for annual or perennial crop planning. In progress. Annual/perennial rules, suggestions, and cycle-window validation exist. Reusable templates, weather context, and automatic preparation/replanting tasks are not implemented. FR-22 Approve fertilizer plans and record planned products, doses, units, quantities, dates/recurrence, coverage, and actual applications. Implemented for the current workflow. Farm product catalogs, approvals, idempotent recurrence tasks, partial applications, evidence, and follow-up are available. Inventory and cost controls are not. See fertilization. FR-23 Connect pest/disease observations, follow-up, intervention criteria, and approved treatment workflows to affected areas. Partial. Structured inspections, catalog, follow-ups, manual preventive assignments, and treatments exist. Risk criteria and a formal treatment approval workflow do not. FR-24 Plan and execute irrigation; record moisture, sources, quality, infrastructure, balances, maintenance, and sensor checks. Implemented for operational records. Includes recurring schedules and idempotent occurrence generation. Approval, weather integration, complete hydraulic mapping, and some administrative permission policies remain gaps. See water management. FR-25 Store soil/foliar results and use them as context for reviewed agronomic plans. Partial. Structured nutrients, configurable profiles, provisional interpretation, technical review, and plan linkage exist. Lab integration, method-aware confidence, and recommendations do not. FR-26 Plan harvest work and record harvested quantities, quality, and yield against a target and period. Partial. Editable structured harvest records link to field/block/row and crop cycle with quantities, units, quality, rejection, and variance. Separate harvest planning and bunch-level traceability are missing. FR-27 Generate work from approved plans, recurrence, crop stages, and observations without duplicates. Partial. Fertilizer and irrigation occurrence generation is idempotent; planting tasks can be generated manually from approved plans. Calendar/stage-driven crop work and observation alerts are not implemented. FR-28 Notify workers and supervisors about assignments, overdue tasks, and blockers. Planned. Sensor threshold alerts exist, but task notifications and external delivery channels do not. FR-29 Capture field work offline and synchronize it later with visible status and conflict handling. Planned. The frontend is not an offline-first/PWA client; demo browser storage is not synchronization."},{"title":"Event types","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--event-types","text":"Event types /api/v1/events supports these historical record types: Type Use --- --- SCOUTING OBSERVATION Crop inspection or suspected pest/disease observation TREATMENT APPLICATION Treatment activity FERTILIZER APPLICATION Fertilization activity IRRIGATION EVENT Irrigation activity HANDLING EVENT Crop handling activity SOIL SAMPLE Soil sampling activity HARVEST EVENT Legacy/general harvest activity Structured harvest is stored separately as a HARVEST monitoring record linked to a crop cycle. Generic event history remains available for compatibility."},{"title":"Non-functional requirements","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--non-functional-requirements","text":"Non-functional requirements The following target values are proposals only. They need agreement against deployment budget, expected farms/users, data volumes, and operating conditions. ID Requirement Evidence and remaining work Status --- --- --- --- NFR-01 Protect credentials and authenticated sessions. BCrypt password hashes, HTTP-only same-site cookies, CSRF protection, login session/token rotation, and an eight-hour idle timeout exist. Production needs HTTPS/secure cookies; abuse protection, recovery, and verification are missing. Partial NFR-02 Enforce farm-level data and role isolation on every operation. Membership and role checks are used across the current APIs and cross-farm cases have automated coverage. Some domain-specific management permission policies remain incomplete. Partial NFR-03 Preserve data integrity and atomic writes. Database constraints, transactions, validation, and version checks protect selected workflows. Broader geometry, measurement, plant-range, and cross-module integrity rules need definition and coverage. Partial NFR-04 Keep common worker operations responsive under expected load. No load benchmark exists. Proposed target: p95 ≤ 2 seconds for login, daily-task listing, and task submission with 50 concurrent active users on a documented deployment, excluding file transfer. Proposed target NFR-05 Bound resource use as agricultural history grows. Spatial and relation/time indexes exist. Collection endpoints are not consistently paginated, and representative growth/load benchmarks are absent. Partial NFR-06 Provide predictable service availability. /actuator/health exists; uptime is not measured and no highly available deployment is configured. Proposed target: 99.5% monthly availability after measurement and operating rules are agreed. Proposed target NFR-07 Recover agricultural data after infrastructure failure. Docker persists a local database volume but does not provide backup. Scheduled backups and restore exercises are not configured. Proposed starting targets: RPO ≤ 24 hours and RTO ≤ 4 hours. Partial NFR-08 Preserve or clearly recover worker sessions during deployment. Sessions use the API process's in-memory store and disappear on restart. Multiple API instances require shared sessions or a deliberate reauthentication strategy. Partial NFR-09 Avoid duplicate work and stale concurrent updates. Version checks reject stale task and domain edits; recurring fertilizer and irrigation generation are idempotent. General request idempotency and offline conflict resolution are missing. Partial NFR-10 Record accountable operational history. Events and workflow records retain authors and timestamps; selected decisions are versioned. A unified immutable audit log for sign-in, membership, edits, deletion, and all workflow transitions is missing. Partial NFR-11 Make failures diagnosable and API errors predictable. Health reporting, application logging, and authentication/access status codes exist. Standard error bodies, correlation IDs, service metrics, and alerting are incomplete. Partial NFR-12 Keep changes maintainable and verifiable. Domain packages, Flyway migrations, Gradle wrapper, OpenAPI, backend tests, frontend unit tests, and Playwright workflows exist. CI enforcement and consistent coverage of each new workflow remain to be established. Partial NFR-13 Support understandable, accessible field workflows. The Spanish UI is responsive and includes labeled controls and keyboard-accessible dialogs; desktop/mobile browser flows are tested. Worker usability research and a full accessibility audit remain outstanding. Partial NFR-14 Handle dates, time zones, and units consistently. Events use instants, task/planting workflows use dates and times, and many domain measurements carry explicit units. Farm time zones and broader unit-pair validation are missing. Partial NFR-15 Deploy reproducibly with environment-specific configuration. Docker Compose, Flyway, and Spring environment overrides support local setup. Production packaging, secret management, deployment automation, and shared session storage are not supplied. Partial NFR-16 Minimize and protect personal and operational data. Farm authorization is implemented and photo content is access-controlled in PostgreSQL. Retention, account deletion/export policy, backup access, and formal data lifecycle controls remain undefined. Partial No performance, availability, accessibility, recovery, or security certification is implied by the implementation or automated tests. Production targets must be measured and accepted against a defined deployment."},{"title":"Authorization model","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--authorization-model","text":"Authorization model Roles are assigned independently for each farm. The broad responsibilities are: Role Current responsibility --- --- ADMIN Manage farm membership and roles; administer farm operations. SUPERVISOR Manage field work and operational workflows within the farm. AGRONOMIST Use agronomic configuration, review, and approval workflows; does not administer team membership. WORKER Read permitted farm information, execute assigned work, and submit field records allowed by the module. This is not a substitute for endpoint authorization. Exact create, edit, review, and delete permissions vary by module and are enforced by the API. The module docs and tests are authoritative for each workflow; the remaining gaps are tracked in FR-05 and the module audits."},{"title":"Related documentation","anchor":"doc-docs-system-requirements-md--related-documentation","text":"Related documentation - Repository overview and local setup - Crop-management requirements and endpoint inventory - Fertilization workflow and limits - Water-management workflow and API - Applied agronomic guidance"}]},{"title":"Crop Management Requirements Audit","group":"Requirements and design","page":"crop-management.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--crop-management-requirements-audit","text":"Last reviewed: 2026-11-21 . This document audits crop management only : crop master data, varieties, planting cycles, lote evaluation, planting material, planting plans, field execution, establishment, phenology, regional calendars, and agronomist access. Nutrient, water, pest/disease, and production management are separate modules and are intentionally excluded from this audit."},{"title":"Overall coverage","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--overall-coverage","text":"Overall coverage Estimated functional coverage: approximately 80%. The end-to-end crop workflow is usable. The remaining work is concentrated in automation, richer catalog data, fine-grained permissions, and unified auditability. This is a product-completeness estimate, not an automated test-coverage percentage."},{"title":"Status legend","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--status-legend","text":"Status legend - Implemented — usable end-to-end functionality exists in the API, persistence, UI, and/or workflow where applicable. - In progress — the core capability exists, but important behavior or controls remain incomplete. - Missing — there is no usable end-to-end implementation yet. - Planned — missing work identified for a future delivery."},{"title":"Summary","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--summary","text":"Summary Capability Status Current coverage Remaining gap --- --- --- --- Crop master catalog Implemented Farm-scoped crop records, codes, names, active state, and catalog UI. Richer agronomic reference attributes and complete lifecycle editing/archive. Crop varieties In progress Varieties are associated with parent crops and selectable in planting workflows. Seed/source, maturity, resistance, supplier data, and full lifecycle management. Planting cycles In progress Cycles store block/row, crop/variety, planting window, expected harvest, current stage, and stage history. Planned area and density belong to associated planting plans; actual area and plant counts belong to execution records. Cycle-level area reconciliation, automatic stage transitions, replant links, and planned-versus-actual lifecycle reconciliation. Site/lote evaluation Implemented Assessment creation, review, approval, and rejection are available in the cycle workflow. More rule-based risk validation and richer assessment history. Planting material Implemented Material lots can be registered and accepted or rejected before planting. Complete lot-document lifecycle and stronger catalog validation. Planting plan and execution Implemented Area, density, responsible person, approval, task creation, and execution are available. Calendar- and stage-driven scheduling and broader task automation. Establishment and phenology In progress Checks record inspected, surviving, dead, and replaced plant counts; dated stage observations and history are available. Structured resowing follow-up, automatic replacement work, stage progression, alerts, and stronger cycle reconciliation. Regional calendars In progress Annual/perennial rules, suggestions, preparation lead time, and cycle-window validation exist. Reusable templates, weather context, and automatic work generation. Agronomist access In progress AGRONOMIST role, agronomic access checks, configuration access, and approvals exist. Capability-based, delegated, time-bound, regional, or module-specific permissions. Crop scheduling automation Missing Approved planting plans can create tasks manually. Automatic preparation, planting, replanting, and stage-driven task generation. Crop audit trail Missing Farm, block, cycle, user, approval, and media references exist. Unified immutable history of crop record changes and decisions."},{"title":"Detailed requirements matrix","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--detailed-requirements-matrix","text":"Detailed requirements matrix ID Requirement Status Implemented evidence Gap or next work --- --- --- --- --- CM-01 Store farm-scoped crop master records with code, name, and active state. Implemented V14 crop variety catalog.sql, CropCatalogController, CropCatalogService, and catalog UI. Add richer agronomic attributes and shared reference-data support. CM-02 Store varieties and associate each variety with its parent crop. In progress Crop/variety persistence and API/UI association exist. Add maturity, resistance, source, supplier, edit, archive, and approval lifecycle. CM-03 Associate blocks, planting cycles, and material lots with crop and variety records. Implemented Catalog identifiers are wired into block, cycle, and material-lot workflows. Gradually make catalog references mandatory for legacy-compatible records. CM-04 Record a crop cycle by block with crop, variety, dates, area, expected harvest, and growth stage. In progress PlantingDtos, PlantingController, cycle persistence, and the Planting page provide the core flow. The cycle stores planting dates, expected harvest, current stage, and stage history; planned area/density are held by planting plans, and actual area/plant counts by execution records. Add a cycle-level view and reconciliation of planned versus actual area and dates, automatic stage changes, and replant links. CM-05 Evaluate a lot/site before planting, including risks and technical approval. Implemented Site-assessment creation, review, approval, and rejection are available from cycle details. Add rule-based risk validation and more complete assessment history. CM-06 Register planting-material lots and accept or reject them before use. Implemented Material-lot creation and decision endpoints are available in the planting workflow. Complete document/certificate lifecycle and stronger lot-to-cycle traceability. CM-07 Create, approve, and execute a planting plan with area, density, responsible person, and target window. Implemented Planting-plan persistence, approval/cancellation, task creation, execution records, and UI controls exist. Add reusable plan templates and automatic scheduling from calendar rules. CM-08 Record establishment checks, survival, mortality, replacement, and resowing follow-up. In progress Establishment-check endpoint and cycle follow-up UI store inspected, surviving, dead, and replaced plant counts with dated notes. Add structured resowing follow-up, automatic replacement work generation, count reconciliation, and cycle-level alerts. CM-09 Record dated phenological stage observations for each cycle. Implemented Stage endpoint, stage history, and Planting page controls are available. Add automatic progression rules and stage-based task triggers. CM-10 Maintain regional annual and perennial planting calendars by region, crop, and optional variety. In progress V15 agronomist role and planting calendars.sql, calendar API, suggestions, and Settings UI exist. Add reusable regional templates, weather-aware recommendations, and automatic preparation/replanting tasks. CM-11 Give agronomists agronomic configuration and approval access without team administration. In progress AGRONOMIST role, FarmAccess.requireAgronomic, module checks, and role-aware Settings UI exist. Add capability-level permissions, delegation, expiry, and region/module scope. CM-12 Generate crop-management tasks automatically from calendars, stages, approved plans, and resowing needs. Missing A manager can manually generate one task from an approved planting plan; repeating the request returns the plan's existing task. No calendar-, stage-, or resowing-triggered scheduler exists. Build automatic, idempotent, dependency-aware generation with overdue/escalation handling. CM-13 Maintain a unified audit history for crop records, approvals, and workflow decisions. Missing Individual records retain farm/block/cycle/user references and media links. Add immutable change history and a cycle-level audit timeline."},{"title":"Implemented user-facing workflow","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--implemented-user-facing-workflow","text":"Implemented user-facing workflow The Planting page currently supports: 1. Create and view a crop cycle. 2. Evaluate and approve/reject the site or lot. 3. Register and accept/reject planting material. 4. Create and approve the planting plan. 5. Generate work and record field execution. 6. Record establishment and replacement follow-up. 7. Update and review phenological stages. Crop and variety catalogs and regional calendars are managed from the Settings area. The main crop-management API groups are /api/v1/catalog/crops, /api/v1/planting, and /api/v1/planting-calendars."},{"title":"Complete endpoint inventory","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--complete-endpoint-inventory","text":"Complete endpoint inventory The following table lists every endpoint owned by the crop catalog, planting, and regional-calendar controllers. All routes require an authenticated session and farm access; write routes also require CSRF protection."},{"title":"Crop catalog","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--crop-catalog","text":"Crop catalog Method Endpoint Purpose --- --- --- GET /api/v1/catalog/crops?farmId={farmId} List the farm's crops and varieties. POST /api/v1/catalog/crops Create a farm crop. POST /api/v1/catalog/crops/{cropId}/varieties Create a variety under a crop."},{"title":"Planting workflow","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--planting-workflow","text":"Planting workflow Method Endpoint Purpose --- --- --- GET /api/v1/planting?farmId={farmId} List cycles and their planting workflow data for a farm. POST /api/v1/planting/cycles Create a planting cycle. POST /api/v1/planting/cycles/{id}/site-assessment Record a site/lote assessment. POST /api/v1/planting/cycles/{id}/site-assessment/approve Approve a site assessment. POST /api/v1/planting/cycles/{id}/site-assessment/reject Reject a site assessment. POST /api/v1/planting/material-lots Register planting material. POST /api/v1/planting/material-lots/{id}/decision Accept or reject planting material. POST /api/v1/planting/plans Create a planting plan. POST /api/v1/planting/plans/{id}/approve Approve a planting plan. POST /api/v1/planting/plans/{id}/cancel Cancel a planting plan. POST /api/v1/planting/plans/{id}/task Generate a field task from an approved plan. POST /api/v1/planting/plans/{id}/executions Record field execution of a planting plan. POST /api/v1/planting/cycles/{id}/establishment-checks Record establishment, survival, mortality, or replacement follow-up. POST /api/v1/planting/cycles/{id}/stages Record a phenological stage observation."},{"title":"Regional planting calendars","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--regional-planting-calendars","text":"Regional planting calendars Method Endpoint Purpose --- --- --- GET /api/v1/planting-calendars?farmId={farmId} List calendar rules for a farm. GET /api/v1/planting-calendars/suggestions?farmId={farmId}&cropId={cropId}[&varietyId={varietyId}&year={year}] Resolve applicable planting-window suggestions. POST /api/v1/planting-calendars Create a calendar rule. PUT /api/v1/planting-calendars/{id} Update a calendar rule. DELETE /api/v1/planting-calendars/{id} Deactivate a calendar rule. The planting controller currently returns workflow data from the farm-level list route. There are no separate GET-by-id, update, or delete routes yet for cycles, material lots, planting plans, executions, establishment checks, or stage observations. Crop and variety records also have create/list routes only; full catalog lifecycle endpoints are part of the in-progress work."},{"title":"Planned next work","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--planned-next-work","text":"Planned next work 1. Complete crop and variety catalog lifecycle and agronomic reference attributes. 2. Automate stage transitions and calendar-driven preparation, planting, and resowing tasks. 3. Add delegated and time-bound agronomist permissions. 4. Add unified crop-cycle approvals, history, and change traceability."},{"title":"Related documentation","anchor":"doc-docs-crop-management-requirements-md--related-documentation","text":"Related documentation - README.md — current repository overview, local setup, feature coverage, and API areas. - docs/system-requirements.md — cross-product functional and non-functional requirements. - docs/guias-agronomicas-aplicadas.md — agronomic guidance and operational context."}]},{"title":"Guías agronómicas aplicadas en Agro Core","group":"Requirements and design","page":"agronomic-guides.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--guías-agronómicas-aplicadas-en-agro-core","text":"Fecha de revisión: 21 de noviembre de 2026 ."},{"title":"Propósito","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--propósito","text":"Propósito Este documento registra de dónde surgieron las principales decisiones agronómicas del proyecto, cómo se tradujeron a funciones del software y qué partes siguen pendientes. Su objetivo es conservar trazabilidad entre las fuentes técnicas y la implementación, no reemplazar el criterio de un ingeniero agrónomo ni los protocolos vigentes de la autoridad sanitaria. Las páginas citadas son las páginas del archivo PDF mostradas por el visor. Pueden diferir de la numeración impresa dentro del documento."},{"title":"Estados utilizados","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--estados-utilizados","text":"Estados utilizados Estado Significado --- --- Implementado Existe modelo persistente, API y flujo de interfaz para datos reales. Parcial Existe una parte útil, pero falta trazabilidad, especialización o cobertura del proceso. Planificado La fuente ya fue analizada y existe una propuesta concreta, pero todavía no está implementada. No adoptado La recomendación no se convirtió en regla del sistema por seguridad, vigencia, jurisdicción o falta de validación técnica."},{"title":"Jerarquía de las fuentes","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--jerarquía-de-las-fuentes","text":"Jerarquía de las fuentes Cuando dos documentos difieren, el proyecto aplica este criterio: 1. Para asuntos fitosanitarios en Colombia se priorizan las instrucciones vigentes del ICA y las decisiones de un profesional autorizado. 2. Los manuales de banano de otros países se usan para diseñar procesos, registros y trazabilidad, pero no para copiar automáticamente obligaciones legales, dosis, umbrales o productos. 3. Las guías técnicas regionales sirven como contexto agronómico. Sus valores deben configurarse por cultivo, variedad, suelo, clima y sistema productivo. 4. Los artículos divulgativos se usan únicamente como referencia complementaria y nunca como fuente normativa ni como motor de prescripción."},{"title":"Fuentes revisadas","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--fuentes-revisadas","text":"Fuentes revisadas"},{"title":"1. Cartilla fitosanitaria del ICA","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--1-cartilla-fitosanitaria-del-ica","text":"1. Cartilla fitosanitaria del ICA Fuente: Manejo fitosanitario del cultivo del plátano (Musa spp.) — Medidas para la temporada invernal, ICA, Colombia, 2012. Secciones utilizadas: - Páginas PDF 8–10: prevención, monitoreo e intervención dentro del MIPE. - Página PDF 15: historial del lote, material sano, bioseguridad, sospechas de moko, seguimiento y confirmación por el ICA. - Páginas PDF 29–30: prevención y monitoreo de Sigatoka, incluida la necesidad de método de evaluación y georreferenciación. - Páginas PDF 35–38: observación y conteo de picudos antes de decidir una intervención. - Páginas PDF 39–41: síntomas que pueden confundirse con deficiencias nutricionales y necesidad de análisis de raíz y suelo para nematodos. - Página PDF 46: formato con fecha, plantas monitoreadas, plantas afectadas, incidencia, severidad y observación. Aplicación en el proyecto: - Implementado: inspecciones estructuradas con ubicación, fecha, muestra, plantas afectadas, área afectada en hectáreas, incidencia calculada, severidad, método, síntomas y próxima visita. - Implementado: separación entre hallazgo sospechado, confirmado o descartado; una observación de campo no se presenta como diagnóstico confirmado. - Implementado: revisiones por ADMIN, SUPERVISOR o AGRONOMIST, seguimientos enlazados, tratamientos registrados, evidencias, fotografías y conservación de registros anulados. Registrar un tratamiento no implica un flujo de aprobación. - Implementado: catálogo compartido de Sigatoka, moko, Fusarium, bacteriosis, picudos y nematodos. Los roles de gestión agronómica pueden crear, editar y desactivar problemas; las inspecciones conservan la referencia histórica. El catálogo no está particionado por finca. - Parcial: la ubicación llega a bloque y surco; todavía no se almacena el punto o polígono exacto de cada foco fitosanitario. - Planificado: resultados de laboratorio, cadena de muestras, zonas de contención y registro de notificación a la autoridad. - No adoptado: dosis, mezclas, radios de erradicación y tratamientos descritos en la cartilla no se convierten en acciones automáticas. El documento es de 2012 y cada intervención debe validarse contra el protocolo actual del ICA. Evidencia de implementación: - Migración de inspecciones - Tratamientos y evidencias - Fotografías - Servicio de inspecciones - Pantalla de inspecciones"},{"title":"2. Manual técnico colombiano de cultivo de plátano","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--2-manual-técnico-colombiano-de-cultivo-de-plátano","text":"2. Manual técnico colombiano de cultivo de plátano Fuente: Manual técnico de cultivo de plátano, IES CINOC, Colombia, primera edición, febrero de 2025. Secciones utilizadas: - Páginas PDF 8–11: clima, agua, sombra, viento, topografía, drenaje, suelo e historial del terreno. - Páginas PDF 12–17: selección del material, trazado, densidad, ahoyado y momento de siembra. - Páginas PDF 18–20: manejo inicial, deshije y sucesión madre–hija–nieta. - Páginas PDF 25–27: fertilización basada en análisis de suelo y etapa del cultivo. - Páginas PDF 28–42: manejo integrado de enfermedades y plagas. - Página PDF 43: cosecha según edad del racimo y especificación del mercado. Aplicación en el proyecto: - Implementado: análisis de suelo y foliar, planificación de fertilización por etapa, registro de aplicaciones y seguimiento posterior. - Implementado en primera fase: catálogos de cultivo/variedad, ciclos por bloque, evaluación y aprobación del sitio, lotes de material vegetal, planes y ejecuciones de siembra, controles de establecimiento y observaciones fechadas de etapa. - Parcial: la primera fase no cubre todavía sucesión madre–hija–nieta, fenología detallada, seguimiento individual de racimos ni un historial unificado del ciclo. - No adoptado: distancias, densidades, calendarios, cantidades de fertilización y tratamientos químicos del manual no son valores predeterminados del sistema. - No adoptado: las menciones a clorpirifos, aldicarb y otros tratamientos no se ofrecen como recomendaciones. Requieren validación legal, etiqueta vigente y aprobación profesional. Evidencia de implementación o base actual: - Flujo de ciclos y siembra - Migración del flujo de ciclos productivos - Migración del flujo de producción y cosecha - Pantalla de cosecha y producción - Auditoría de gestión de cultivos - Registros genéricos de cultivo - Flujo de fertilización - Guía detallada de fertilización"},{"title":"3. Manual BPA de banano de Bolivia","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--3-manual-bpa-de-banano-de-bolivia","text":"3. Manual BPA de banano de Bolivia Fuente: Manual de Buenas Prácticas Agrícolas (BPA) en el cultivo de banano (Musa AAA), Ministerio de Desarrollo Rural y Tierras y entidades asociadas, Bolivia. Secciones utilizadas: - Páginas PDF 18–19: procedencia, calidad y protección del agua de riego. - Páginas PDF 25–29: flujo productivo, historial del terreno, lotes, drenaje, material vegetal, plantación, mortalidad, reposición y manejo de población. - Páginas PDF 30–31: análisis de suelo, análisis foliar, plan de fertilización y documentación de aplicaciones. - Páginas PDF 32–33: protección del racimo, cinta semanal, edad, calibre y cosecha. - Páginas PDF 33–36: prevención, observación e intervención dentro del MIP. - Páginas PDF 47–49: cuaderno de campo y trazabilidad hacia atrás, interna y hacia adelante. - Páginas PDF 58–66: ejemplos de registros de labores, Sigatoka, fertilización, plaguicidas, racimos y cosecha. Aplicación en el proyecto: - Implementado en riego: sector, sistema, fuente de agua, área, caudal, medición de humedad, profundidad, instrumento, lluvia, condición del drenaje, programación, responsable y ejecución real. - Implementado en riego: volumen medido por contador o estimado por caudal, duración efectiva, interrupciones y justificación de desviaciones. - Implementado en fertilización: muestra, laboratorio, método, plan aprobado, producto, composición, dosis, cobertura, lote del producto, aplicación parcial y seguimiento. - Implementado en sanidad: inspección, revisión, tratamiento, seguimiento, evidencia y autor de cada registro. - Implementado transversalmente: códigos de finca/campo/bloque/surco, autor, fechas, tareas y eventos para conservar el historial de campo. - Implementado en siembra: ciclo por bloque, evaluación/aprobación del sitio, lote de material vegetal, plan aprobado, tarea, ejecución y controles de establecimiento/supervivencia. Las guías sirven como contexto; sus umbrales y densidades no se aplican automáticamente. - Implementado en cosecha: registro estructurado vinculado a campo, bloque o surco y ciclo, con fecha, área, rendimiento estimado, cantidad, unidad, calidad, rechazo y motivo; se puede consultar y editar. - Parcial en ciclo/cosecha: faltan trazabilidad individual de racimos, edad y calibre, sucesión y cierre productivo completo. - No adoptado: el umbral de mortalidad del 1 %, las distancias 2,5 × 2,5 m y 2,7 × 2,7 m, las cuarentenas y las frecuencias del manual se tratarán como parámetros configurables, no como reglas universales. - No adoptado: registros, productos y obligaciones propios de SENASAG no se presentan como requisitos legales colombianos. Evidencia de implementación: - Migración del flujo de riego - Migración de agua, infraestructura y mantenimiento - Migración de recurrencias de riego - Servicio de riego - Pantalla de riego - Guía detallada de gestión del agua - Migración del flujo de fertilización - Pantalla de fertilización"},{"title":"4. Guía Banano y Biodiversidad","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--4-guía-banano-y-biodiversidad","text":"4. Guía Banano y Biodiversidad Fuente: Recomendaciones para mejorar la protección de la biodiversidad en los cultivos de banano, Global Nature Fund, GIZ, Fundación Humedales, ANDI, Uniban y Fundación Uniban, estado octubre de 2024. Secciones utilizadas: - Páginas PDF 9–11: línea base, plan de acción, indicadores y mapa de áreas productivas, no productivas, linderos, árboles, hábitats y corredores. - Páginas PDF 14–15: protección de ecosistemas acuáticos, zonas de amortiguación e inspección de especies invasoras. - Páginas PDF 21–25: análisis de suelo, documentación de nutrientes, cobertura, erosión, compactación y plan de fertilización. - Páginas PDF 26–31: MIP, monitoreo, medidas preventivas, material certificado y documentación de controles y plaguicidas. - Páginas PDF 34–36: reducción y trazabilidad del uso de agua, permisos, calidad, contador o caudal y mapa de infraestructura hídrica. - Páginas PDF 38–40: clima y agrobiodiversidad. Aplicación en el proyecto: - Implementado: capa de nutrientes basada en la última muestra de suelo por bloque, conservando valor, unidad, método, laboratorio y fecha. - Implementado con límite: la capa muestra una interpretación preliminar bajo/en rango/alto con el perfil provisional incorporado para suelo de banano/plátano; todavía no usa los perfiles personalizados de cada finca. Cada estado exige revisión técnica y no se convierte en una dosis. La pantalla de análisis real sí usa el perfil activo configurado en el API. - Implementado: capa de humedad por sector con unidad, profundidad, origen, vigencia y límites configurados con fundamento técnico. - Implementado: documentación del consumo real o estimado de agua por ejecución. - Implementado: sensores de humedad y temperatura con alertas configurables; los umbrales pertenecen a la finca y no se presentan como universales. - Parcial: existen polígonos de campos y bloques y líneas de surcos, pero todavía no hay capas de fuentes de agua, válvulas, drenajes, zonas de amortiguación, hábitats o corredores. - Parcial: el flujo de siembra registra evaluación de uso anterior, vecinos, suelo, pendiente, drenaje, inundación, viento, contaminación, riesgo fitosanitario y restricciones ambientales, con decisión técnica. No calcula riesgo ni aplica exclusiones geográficas, evaluación de erosión/compactación o línea base de biodiversidad. - Parcial: se guardan fuentes y análisis de calidad de agua, activos hidráulicos, balances y mantenimiento. Siguen pendientes permisos, descargas y eficiencia hídrica por unidad producida. - No adoptado: porcentajes de hábitat, anchos de amortiguación y listas de sustancias no se convierten en cumplimiento legal automático; primero deben contrastarse con la norma aplicable y la certificación elegida. Evidencia de implementación: - Analítica geoespacial - Mapa y capas - Capa de nutrientes - Capa de humedad - Telemetría y alertas"},{"title":"5. Guía divulgativa de plagas y enfermedades 2026","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--5-guía-divulgativa-de-plagas-y-enfermedades-2026","text":"5. Guía divulgativa de plagas y enfermedades 2026 Fuente: Plagas y enfermedades en el cultivo del banano — Guía 2026, Portal Frutícola, 4 de agosto de 2026. Secciones consultadas: - Páginas PDF 2–7: Sigatoka, Fusarium/TR4, moko, virosis, picudo, nematodos y principios de diagnóstico/MIP. - Páginas PDF 8–10: prevención, material certificado, monitoreo, cuarentena y referencias utilizadas por el artículo. Aplicación en el proyecto: - Uso complementario: sirvió para contrastar que el catálogo inicial cubriera problemas relevantes y para reforzar la separación entre observación visual y confirmación técnica o de laboratorio. - Uso complementario: reforzó el énfasis en detección temprana, material sano, bioseguridad y seguimiento. - No adoptado como autoridad: es una captura de un artículo web, no un protocolo oficial. No se toman de allí dosis, frecuencias, umbrales, erradicaciones ni reglas automáticas. Ante diferencias, prevalece la autoridad fitosanitaria vigente."},{"title":"Aplicación por módulo","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--aplicación-por-módulo","text":"Aplicación por módulo"},{"title":"Inspecciones, plagas y enfermedades","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--inspecciones-plagas-y-enfermedades","text":"Inspecciones, plagas y enfermedades Idea técnica Fuente principal Aplicación actual Estado --- --- --- --- Inspección sistemática ICA pp. 8 y 46; BPA pp. 33–35 Fecha, bloque/surco, problema, síntomas, muestra y responsable Implementado Distinguir incidencia y severidad ICA pp. 8 y 46 Incidencia calculada con afectadas/inspeccionadas; severidad y método separados Implementado No confirmar solo por síntomas ICA pp. 15 y 39–40; Guía 2026 pp. 7–9 Estado inicial sospechado y revisión confirmada/descartada Implementado Seguimiento del mismo hallazgo ICA p. 15; BPA pp. 33–35 Próxima visita e inspección hija con misma ubicación/problema Implementado Registrar la intervención sin borrar la observación ICA pp. 8–10; BPA pp. 47–49 Tratamientos vinculados, autor, fecha y cantidad/unidad opcionales Implementado Evidencia de campo Principio BPA de trazabilidad, pp. 47–49 Referencias documentales y fotos JPEG/PNG autenticadas Implementado Visualización territorial ICA p. 30 Estado sanitario por bloque y observaciones históricas por surco Parcial Punto del foco, laboratorio y aviso oficial ICA pp. 15–16 y 39–40 Aún no existe cadena de muestra ni trámite con ICA Planificado El sistema deliberadamente no recomienda tratamientos a partir del nombre de una plaga. Primero registra evidencia; después una persona autorizada revisa y documenta la decisión."},{"title":"Fertilización","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--fertilización","text":"Fertilización Idea técnica Fuente principal Aplicación actual Estado --- --- --- --- Basar el plan en análisis Manual técnico pp. 10 y 25; BPA pp. 30–31; Biodiversidad p. 21 Muestras de suelo/foliares enlazadas al plan Implementado Conservar método y trazabilidad Biodiversidad pp. 21 y 25 Laboratorio, referencia, profundidad, coordenadas, método e informe PDF Implementado Considerar etapa y contexto sanitario ICA pp. 8–9, 29–30 y 39; Manual técnico pp. 25–27 Etapa del cultivo e inspección opcional del mismo bloque Implementado Separar plan de aplicación real BPA pp. 47–49 Aprobación, tarea y aplicaciones parciales con lote y cobertura Implementado Verificar el resultado Biodiversidad pp. 21–25 Fecha de seguimiento, observaciones y nueva muestra opcional Implementado Balance anual de nutrientes Biodiversidad pp. 21 y 25 No existe cálculo entrada/salida ni extracción por cosecha Planificado El sistema solo convierte una dosis aprobada a cantidad total. El perfil base ayuda a identificar resultados que requieren revisión, pero no confirma deficiencias, no calcula necesidades del cultivo y no prescribe mezclas. Los detalles completos están en la guía del módulo de fertilización."},{"title":"Riego y humedad del suelo","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--riego-y-humedad-del-suelo","text":"Riego y humedad del suelo Idea técnica Fuente principal Aplicación actual Estado --- --- --- --- Identificar sistema, sector y fuente BPA p. 18; Biodiversidad p. 35 Sector con bloque, área, sistema, fuente y caudal opcional Implementado Medir con contexto ICA pp. 7–9; Manual técnico pp. 9–11 Valor, unidad, profundidad, instrumento, lluvia y drenaje Implementado Usar límites técnicamente sustentados Biodiversidad p. 36 Rango configurable, fundamento, vigencia y revisiones versionadas Implementado Planificar y asignar BPA pp. 47–49 Fecha/hora, duración, volumen, responsable, justificación e instrucciones Implementado Documentar agua utilizada Biodiversidad p. 35 Contador o caudal estimado, duración efectiva e interrupciones Implementado Controlar desviaciones Principio BPA de trazabilidad Motivo obligatorio cuando se supera duración o volumen programado Implementado Integrar sensores Necesidad de monitoreo continuo Lecturas asociables a sector, alertas por umbral y registros de verificación/calibración Implementado en registros; falta conector de campo/IoT Calidad, permisos e infraestructura hídrica BPA pp. 18–19; Biodiversidad pp. 34–36 Fuentes, análisis de calidad, activos hidráulicos, coordenadas, balances, mantenimiento y verificaciones de sensores Implementado en registros operativos; permisos y mapa hidráulico completo pendientes Los límites de humedad son definidos por el técnico en la unidad del sector (% volumétrico, % gravimétrico o kPa). El modo demo contiene valores ficticios y los identifica expresamente como no agronómicos."},{"title":"Mapas, sensores y alertas","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--mapas-sensores-y-alertas","text":"Mapas, sensores y alertas Actualmente existen estas capas: 1. Plano de la finca: campos, bloques y surcos georreferenciados. 2. Salud del cultivo: hallazgos activos, evaluación reciente sin hallazgos o falta de evaluación reciente. No equivale a certificar todo el bloque. 3. Humedad del suelo: condición de cada sector respecto de sus propios límites. Cuando un bloque tiene varios sectores no se inventa un promedio. 4. Nutrientes: disponibilidad del resultado de cada nutriente en la última muestra de suelo. El color no significa deficiencia ni recomendación de dosis. Los sensores admiten humedad del suelo, humedad del aire, temperatura del aire y temperatura del suelo. También hay registros de verificación/calibración. Las reglas de alerta son comparaciones transparentes contra umbrales configurados. No hay un conector de campo/IoT incluido, modelo predictivo ni recomendación agronómica automática. Falta incorporar al mapa: - puntos o polígonos exactos de inspección y focos; - fuentes de agua, pozos, depósitos, válvulas, canales y drenajes; - zonas de amortiguación, exclusión, hábitats y corredores; - lotes de material vegetal, resiembras y estados del ciclo; - modelos validados de aptitud, riesgo o predicción."},{"title":"Siembra y ciclos productivos","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--siembra-y-ciclos-productivos","text":"Siembra y ciclos productivos"},{"title":"Situación actual","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--situación-actual","text":"Situación actual Además de los registros genéricos CROP CYCLE, existe un flujo especializado con catálogo de cultivos y variedades, ciclos por bloque, evaluación/aprobación del sitio, lotes de material vegetal, planes de siembra, ejecuciones, controles de establecimiento y observaciones fechadas de etapas. Los planes aprobados pueden generar tareas de campo. El inventario de endpoints y sus límites está en la auditoría de gestión de cultivos. La cosecha también tiene un registro HARVEST estructurado y asociado al ciclo. Su captura y corrección son operativas; no debe confundirse con seguimiento por racimo ni con cierre automático del ciclo."},{"title":"Flujo de referencia y cobertura actual","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--flujo-de-referencia-y-cobertura-actual","text":"Flujo de referencia y cobertura actual Paso Información y control de referencia Cobertura actual en Agro Core Fuente --- --- --- --- 1. Evaluación del sitio Historial, cultivos previos, vecinos, contaminación, inundación, pendiente, viento, drenaje y restricciones geográficas La evaluación guarda el uso anterior, vecinos, análisis de suelo opcional, pendiente, drenaje, riesgos de inundación/viento/contaminación/sanidad, restricciones ambientales y notas. No calcula riesgos ni aplica exclusiones geográficas. BPA pp. 26–27; Manual técnico pp. 10–11; Biodiversidad pp. 11, 14 y 24 2. Aprobación del terreno Análisis de suelo vigente, riesgos, medidas requeridas y decisión versionada del responsable técnico Una evaluación por ciclo puede aprobarse o rechazarse con motivo y control de versión. No se puede reemplazar ni volver a decidir tras la primera decisión. El análisis de suelo es opcional; el API no exige que esté vigente ni comprueba medidas correctivas. Manual técnico p. 10; Biodiversidad p. 21 3. Lote de material vegetal Cultivo, clon/variedad, tipo, proveedor, origen, certificado, cantidad, inspección sanitaria, decisión y documentos/fotos El lote guarda tipo, proveedor, referencias de origen/certificado, cantidad recibida e inspección sanitaria en texto; aceptación/rechazo guarda cantidades y motivo. No se adjuntan certificados, fotos ni lotes de reposición al lote. BPA p. 28; Manual técnico pp. 12–14; ICA pp. 15, 36 y 39 4. Plan de siembra Geometría, sistema de trazado, distancia, densidad calculada, población objetivo, ventana, área, responsable y justificación El plan guarda sistema, distancias, densidad/población objetivo, ventana, área, responsable y fundamento técnico. Usa la ubicación existente del bloque/surco, sin geometría propia; el responsable genera la tarea manualmente. BPA p. 28; Manual técnico pp. 15–17; Biodiversidad pp. 26–27 5. Ejecución Fecha y área reales, lote vegetal usado, plantas sembradas/rechazadas, responsables, clima/humedad y evidencia Guarda fecha, área, plantas sembradas/rechazadas, ubicación detallada y notas; crea un evento agrícola enlazado. Clima, humedad y evidencia no son campos estructurados de la ejecución. BPA pp. 28 y 47–49 6. Establecimiento Plantas vivas, mortalidad, causas, resiembras, lote de reposición, uniformidad y fotos Los controles guardan plantas revisadas/vivas/muertas/repuestas, uniformidad, causas y notas. Se valida que vivas + muertas no supere revisadas, pero no se exige que las cantidades cuadren. No hay detalle estructurado de resiembra, enlace a lote de reposición ni adjuntos. BPA p. 28 7. Fenología Observaciones fechadas de establecimiento, vegetativo, floración, llenado, cosecha y renovación El usuario registra manualmente una etapa del catálogo actual (PLANNED, ESTABLISHMENT, VEGETATIVE, FLOWERING, FILLING, HARVEST, RENEWAL o CLOSED), fecha y notas, y actualiza el historial. No hay mediciones fenológicas detalladas, adjuntos ni tareas disparadas por etapa. Manual técnico pp. 8–9 y 43; BPA pp. 32–33 8. Sucesión Relación madre–hija–nieta, sucesor seleccionado, deshijes y población efectiva No implementado; no se guardan sucesores ni relaciones de sucesión. Manual técnico pp. 19–20; BPA p. 29 9. Racimo Fecha/semana de aparición, cinta, embolse, edad, calibre y pronóstico revisable de cosecha No implementado; los registros de cosecha no siguen racimos individuales, edad, cinta ni calibre. BPA pp. 32–33 10. Cierre del ciclo Producción, calidad, pérdidas, duración real, razones de desviación y decisión de renovación No hay flujo de cierre de ciclo; los registros estructurados de cosecha se conservan de forma independiente. BPA pp. 33 y 47–49; Manual técnico p. 43"},{"title":"Reglas de diseño acordadas","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--reglas-de-diseño-acordadas","text":"Reglas de diseño acordadas - Diseño objetivo: las etapas cambian mediante observaciones reales con fecha, responsable y evidencia. Hoy se registran fecha, autor y notas, sin adjunto directo a la observación de etapa; una proyección nunca reemplazará una fecha real. - La densidad geométrica puede calcularse, pero debe identificarse como estimación y quedar aprobada por el técnico. - Distancias, densidades, mortalidad aceptable, frecuencias y ventanas serán parámetros configurables por protocolo; no se copiarán como constantes de los manuales. - Diseño objetivo: conservar el historial de ciclos anteriores y corregir con revisiones o anulaciones justificadas. Hoy no hay rutas generales de edición o cancelación de ciclos ni una auditoría unificada; algunas decisiones sí tienen autor, fecha, motivo y control de versión. - El ciclo ya se relaciona con planes/tareas de siembra y con registros de cosecha; su integración completa con inspecciones, fertilización, riego, fotos y cambios de ciclo todavía no está resuelta. - Los mapas mostrarán ciclo, edad, lote vegetal, faltantes y resiembras solo cuando exista información real; Sin dato será distinto de Sin problema."},{"title":"Entidades previstas","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--entidades-previstas","text":"Entidades previstas El alcance implementado en la primera fase usa: - crop cycle - crop cycle site assessment - planting material lot - planting plan - planting execution - planting establishment check - crop cycle stage history La siguiente fase agregará: - phenology observation - production successor - bunch tracking"},{"title":"Orden de implementación","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--orden-de-implementación","text":"Orden de implementación 1. Implementado con límites: ciclo productivo y evaluación/aprobación del sitio; no se exige una muestra de suelo para aprobar. 2. Implementado con límites: lotes de material vegetal y plan de siembra, sin adjuntos documentales al lote ni geometría propia del plan. 3. Implementado en primera fase: ejecuciones, generación manual de tareas, controles de establecimiento y observaciones fechadas de etapa; falta evidencia estructurada en ejecución y seguimiento de resiembra. 4. Siguiente fase: fenología detallada y sucesión madre–hija–nieta. 5. Parcial: cosecha estructurada y vinculada al ciclo; faltan seguimiento del racimo y cierre productivo. 6. Siguiente fase: capas del mapa, indicadores y alertas específicas de ciclos."},{"title":"Cosecha y producción","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--cosecha-y-producción","text":"Cosecha y producción La pantalla Cosecha y producción guarda registros HARVEST estructurados y asociados a un ciclo. Captura ubicación en campo, bloque o surco, fecha, área, rendimiento estimado, cantidad cosechada, unidad, calidad, cantidad y motivo del rechazo, y notas. Los registros se pueden consultar y editar; los totales y la varianza se presentan por unidad seleccionada. Los antiguos eventos HARVEST EVENT y otros registros genéricos se conservan para compatibilidad histórica. Sigue pendiente la trazabilidad por racimo —edad, calibre y pronóstico—, la clasificación vinculada a una ejecución de corte y el cierre del ciclo con pérdidas, duración y decisión de renovación. Las fuentes principales para esa siguiente fase son el BPA de Bolivia, páginas PDF 32–33 y 47–49, y el manual técnico colombiano, página PDF 43."},{"title":"Decisiones que el sistema no debe automatizar","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--decisiones-que-el-sistema-no-debe-automatizar","text":"Decisiones que el sistema no debe automatizar 1. Confirmar una enfermedad únicamente por una fotografía o un síntoma visual. 2. Recomendar o autorizar plaguicidas, fertilizantes o desinfectantes por coincidencia textual con una guía. 3. Copiar dosis, mezclas, frecuencias, radios de erradicación o cuarentenas antiguas o de otra jurisdicción. 4. Clasificar un nutriente como deficiente sin unidad, método, muestra y criterio técnico aplicable. 5. Usar un rango universal de humedad para todos los suelos, profundidades, instrumentos y etapas. 6. Confundir datos de demostración con mediciones reales. 7. Presentar una proyección de etapa, rendimiento o cosecha como observación real. 8. Interpretar ausencia de datos como ausencia de riesgo."},{"title":"Cobertura actual resumida","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--cobertura-actual-resumida","text":"Cobertura actual resumida Módulo Cobertura frente a las guías --- --- Inspecciones fitosanitarias Alta para observación, revisión, seguimiento y evidencia; falta laboratorio, foco exacto y flujo oficial. Fertilización Alta para trazabilidad análisis → plan → aprobación → aplicación → seguimiento; falta balance nutricional anual. Riego Alta para sector → medición → programación → ejecución y volumen; ahora incluye calidad, infraestructura, balance y mantenimiento; faltan permisos y mapa hidráulico completo. Mapas Media: finca, salud, humedad y nutrientes; faltan biodiversidad, agua, drenaje, focos y siembra/ciclos. Sensores y alertas Media: ingestión, umbrales transparentes y registros de verificación/calibración; falta un conector IoT de campo y recomendaciones validadas. Siembra y ciclos Media/alta en la primera fase: catálogo, ciclo, lote, evaluación, aprobación, plan, tarea, ejecución, establecimiento y observaciones de etapa; faltan automatización, fenología avanzada, sucesión y auditabilidad unificada. Cosecha Parcial: registros estructurados editables y vinculados al ciclo, con calidad, rechazo y varianza; faltan trazabilidad por racimo y cierre productivo completo."},{"title":"Mantenimiento de esta guía","anchor":"doc-docs-guias-agronomicas-aplicadas-md--mantenimiento-de-esta-guía","text":"Mantenimiento de esta guía Cada cambio agronómico deberá actualizar este archivo indicando: 1. fuente y página; 2. principio adoptado; 3. decisión de producto; 4. migración, API, pantalla y prueba que lo implementan; 5. límites o partes expresamente no adoptadas; 6. cambio de estado: planificado, parcial o implementado. Una nueva guía no debe modificar automáticamente reglas existentes. Primero debe compararse con las fuentes actuales, la normativa aplicable y el criterio técnico; después se versiona el protocolo y finalmente se implementa."}]},{"title":"Fertilización: diagnóstico → aprobación → aplicación → seguimiento","group":"Module guides","page":"fertilization.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-docs-fertilization-md--fertilización-diagnóstico-aprobación-aplicación-seguimiento","text":"Última revisión: 21 de noviembre de 2026 . La sección Fertilización conserva el historial anterior y agrega tres vistas: - Análisis : muestras de suelo o foliares con bloque, fecha, laboratorio, referencia, profundidad, coordenadas opcionales y resultados N, P, K, pH, materia orgánica, Ca, Mg, S, B y Zn. Cada resultado conserva su unidad y método. Los parámetros no medidos permanecen vacíos. Se puede adjuntar un informe PDF de hasta 10 MB; la descarga exige acceso a la finca. - Planes : diagnóstico técnico, objetivo, etapa, análisis de referencia, inspección sanitaria opcional del mismo bloque, producto y composición, dosis, cobertura, método, fecha/hora, responsable y fecha de seguimiento. Pueden seleccionar un producto del catálogo, validar su unidad estándar y programar una aplicación única, semanal, quincenal o mensual hasta dos años. ADMIN/SUPERVISOR registran y aprueban con justificación y control de versión. La decisión identifica a quien aprobó; no certifica las credenciales profesionales de quien ejecutará la aplicación. No hay edición general de análisis o planes; la revisión técnica de un análisis sí se puede actualizar. Para corregir datos se registra otro análisis o plan. Un plan único puede anularse antes de generar su tarea. Al aprobar un plan recurrente se generan sus ocurrencias y tareas de inmediato; después no se anula para preservar su trazabilidad. - Aplicaciones : desde un plan aprobado con tarea, el responsable asignado o un administrador/supervisor de la finca registra cantidades parciales, fecha real, lote de producto, cobertura, método y notas. En planes recurrentes se selecciona la ocurrencia concreta; cada ocurrencia conserva su propia cantidad aplicada. La aplicación crea un evento agrícola; después se pueden adjuntar fotos JPEG/PNG con el módulo existente. En cada ocurrencia, la suma no puede superar la cantidad aprobada para esa aplicación. La cobertura describe cada pasada y no se suma: dos aplicaciones parciales pueden recorrer el mismo bloque. Una aplicación inicia la tarea, pero no la completa ni sustituye su revisión habitual."},{"title":"Endpoints","anchor":"doc-docs-fertilization-md--endpoints","text":"Endpoints La API directa de fertilización usa la base /api/v1/fertilization y exige sesión, pertenencia a la finca y los permisos correspondientes: Método Ruta Uso --- --- --- GET /fertilization?farmId={id} Consulta análisis, planes, aplicaciones y seguimientos de una finca. POST /fertilization/analyses Crea un análisis de suelo o foliar con resultados de nutrientes. POST /fertilization/analyses/{id}/review Registra o actualiza la revisión técnica de un análisis. POST /fertilization/analyses/{id}/report Adjunta el informe de laboratorio en PDF. GET /fertilization/analyses/{id}/report Descarga el informe de laboratorio. POST /fertilization/plans Crea un plan vinculado a un análisis. POST /fertilization/plans/{id}/approve Aprueba un plan con justificación. POST /fertilization/plans/{id}/cancel Anula un plan con justificación. POST /fertilization/plans/{id}/task Genera la tarea o las ocurrencias de campo del plan aprobado. POST /fertilization/plans/{id}/applications Registra una aplicación real, incluso parcial. POST /fertilization/plans/{id}/followups Registra el seguimiento técnico y un análisis posterior opcional. GET /fertilization/profiles?farmId={id} Consulta los perfiles de referencia activos de la finca. POST /fertilization/profiles Crea un perfil por cultivo, variedad, tipo de muestra y rangos de nutrientes. Requiere rol agronómico. GET /fertilization/products?farmId={id} Consulta el catálogo activo de productos fertilizantes de la finca. POST /fertilization/products Crea un producto con código, composición y unidad estándar. Requiere rol agronómico. Un plan puede incluir productId, recurrence (NONE, WEEKLY, BIWEEKLY o MONTHLY) y recurrenceUntil. Al aprobar un plan recurrente se crean todas sus ocurrencias en fertilization plan task; repetir POST /plans/{id}/task es idempotente. Una aplicación recurrente debe enviar el taskId de su ocurrencia. Endpoints de apoyo: Método Ruta Uso en nutrición --- --- --- GET /api/v1/analytics/map?farmId={id} Devuelve por bloque la última muestra de suelo disponible y sus resultados. GET /api/v1/analytics/dashboard?farmId={id} Incluye cantidades acumuladas de aplicaciones fertilizantes y sus unidades. GET /api/v1/tasks?farmId={id} Lista las tareas generadas desde planes aprobados. PATCH /api/v1/tasks/{id} Actualiza el avance, bloqueo, envío a revisión o finalización de la tarea. POST /api/v1/photos Adjunta fotos de evidencia al evento creado por una aplicación. GET /api/v1/photos?eventId={id} Lista la evidencia de una aplicación. GET /api/v1/photos/{id}/content Consulta o descarga una foto de evidencia."},{"title":"Pantallas","anchor":"doc-docs-fertilization-md--pantallas","text":"Pantallas En modo real, estas pantallas consumen los endpoints anteriores; en modo demo, los datos de fertilización se conservan localmente en el navegador: - Fertilización ( /fertilization): módulo principal con las pestañas: - Análisis : captura N, P, K, pH, materia orgánica, Ca, Mg, S, B y Zn; muestra unidades, métodos, laboratorio, muestra, informe e interpretación preliminar; permite abrir el detalle y descargar o adjuntar el PDF. - Planes : crea, consulta, aprueba, anula y convierte planes aprobados en tareas; muestra diagnóstico, dosis, cobertura, aplicación acumulada, catálogo de productos y frecuencia de aplicación. - Aplicaciones : registra cantidad, fecha, cobertura, método, lote y notas; selecciona la ocurrencia de un plan recurrente, muestra el detalle y permite adjuntar fotos de evidencia. - Perfiles y catálogo : ADMIN, SUPERVISOR y AGRONOMIST pueden crear perfiles activos por finca, cultivo, variedad y muestra, y registrar una decisión técnica confirmada, pendiente de seguimiento o rechazada desde el detalle del análisis; también pueden registrar productos con composición y unidad estándar. - Mapas → Nutrientes ( /maps): selecciona el nutriente y muestra valores por bloque, promedio, fecha de muestra, unidad, método, laboratorio, trazabilidad e interpretación preliminar. También permite comparar nutrientes con humedad. - Dashboard → Nutrientes ( /dashboard): capa resumida de nutrientes en el mapa principal; al seleccionar el mapa se abre la vista completa de Mapas. - Mi trabajo de hoy ( /tasks): muestra las tareas de fertilización generadas desde planes aprobados y permite avanzar, bloquear, enviar a revisión o completar la ejecución según el rol. - Reportes y actividad ( /reports): consulta el evento agrícola creado por una aplicación y su historial general. La interpretación que aparece en Análisis y Mapas es provisional: no confirma por sí sola una deficiencia ni genera recomendaciones, dosis o prescripciones."},{"title":"Cálculos y revisión","anchor":"doc-docs-fertilization-md--cálculos-y-revisión","text":"Cálculos y revisión Solo se convierte la dosis elegida por el técnico a cantidad total: kg/ha × ha, L/ha × ha, g/planta × plantas ÷ 1000 o mL/planta × plantas ÷ 1000. En la vista real de Análisis, el API selecciona el perfil activo de la finca por cultivo, variedad opcional y tipo de muestra, y luego busca rangos por nutriente y unidad. No valida que el método del laboratorio sea compatible con el perfil. La capa de Mapas usa un perfil provisional incorporado para suelo de banano/plátano, no los perfiles personalizados de la finca; sus estados pueden diferir del detalle de Análisis. En demo se usa ese mismo perfil provisional. Todo estado requiere revisión técnica: no confirma una deficiencia ni calcula requerimientos, mezclas o dosis. La composición se transcribe como texto de etiqueta; no se estima absorción. El técnico debe comprobarla con el informe y el contexto del cultivo. Generar tareas es idempotente: repetir la solicitud no duplica la tarea de una aplicación única ni las ocurrencias de un plan recurrente. En un plan recurrente, el total mostrado después de generar tareas es la suma de las cantidades previstas por ocurrencia, mientras cada aplicación se valida contra su propia tarea. Las tareas bloqueadas, en revisión o terminadas no admiten más aplicaciones. El plan muestra programado/aplicado con unidades separadas, vencimiento de revisión y seguimientos con autor, fecha, conclusión y nuevo análisis opcional. Los filtros son por bloque; el CSV exporta planes sin mezclar kg con L. El mapa utiliza la última muestra de suelo registrada por bloque , ordenada por fecha e ID; no exige que incluya todos los nutrientes. Las muestras foliares no sustituyen al suelo. Si la muestra más reciente no midió un nutriente, se muestra «Sin dato» en vez de recuperar silenciosamente un valor antiguo. Se muestran unidades, método, laboratorio, referencia y fecha, y no se promedian muestras históricas. En la vista real de Análisis , la interpretación del API usa el perfil activo de la finca compatible con cultivo, variedad opcional, tipo de muestra, nutriente y unidad; sin perfil o rango compatible muestra «Sin interpretar». No valida la compatibilidad por método de laboratorio. El API permite listar y crear perfiles y productos, pero no editarlos ni desactivarlos; para cambiar un perfil se puede crear otro, que queda priorizado por especificidad y luego por ID descendente. La capa de Mapas → Nutrientes todavía calcula su interpretación en el frontend con un perfil provisional incorporado para suelo de banano/plátano. Por eso, esa capa no debe tomarse como interpretación del perfil personalizado de la finca y puede mostrar un estado distinto al detalle del análisis. En demo se usa el mismo perfil provisional. En todos los casos los colores del mapa representan valores relativos, no una deficiencia ni un óptimo agronómico. Los registros antiguos de monitoreo permanecen conservados, pero al no tener suficiente trazabilidad analítica no se convierten en muestras certificadas."},{"title":"Relación con la cartilla local","anchor":"doc-docs-fertilization-md--relación-con-la-cartilla-local","text":"Relación con la cartilla local Referencia revisada: cartilla-platano-ICA-final-BAJA.pdf, sobre manejo fitosanitario del plátano , no una tabla universal de fertilización de banano. Las páginas PDF 8–9 integran fertilización, riego y drenaje al manejo preventivo; las páginas 29–30 mencionan fertilización adecuada en el manejo de sigatoka; la página 35 relaciona picudos secundarios con desequilibrios nutricionales (incluidos K y B); la página 39 advierte que algunos síntomas radicales pueden parecer deficiencias nutricionales y requieren diagnóstico de laboratorio. Esto motiva conservar contexto sanitario y revisión técnica, no inferir deficiencias ni recomendar dosis o tratamientos a partir de una plaga. Las asociaciones se documentan, no se presentan como causalidad demostrada."},{"title":"Operación y límites","anchor":"doc-docs-fertilization-md--operación-y-límites","text":"Operación y límites Flyway aplica las migraciones V9, V10, V19 y V21 al reiniciar el backend. Los registros, PDF e imágenes se guardan en PostgreSQL; deben incluirse en sus copias de seguridad. Los perfiles de referencia ya son configurables por finca, cultivo, variedad y tipo de muestra; su contenido debe ser validado por el responsable técnico. No hay integración automática con laboratorios, inventario o costos, análisis foliar comparativo ni correlaciones causales con rendimiento o sanidad. El catálogo no controla existencias ni compras y sus perfiles/productos no se pueden editar ni desactivar desde la API actual. Todavía no hay validación específica por método de laboratorio, niveles de confianza ni recomendaciones automáticas de fertilización. La fecha de seguimiento se muestra en el módulo, no envía notificaciones externas. Los permisos se verifican en cada endpoint; archivos PDF se entregan como descarga, no se ejecutan ni se muestran embebidos. No hay escáner antivirus integrado. En modo demo, muestras con datos distintos para cada nutriente, planes, aplicaciones e informes se conservan localmente. Los PDF comparten el límite de almacenamiento del navegador; un error de cuota se comunica sin afirmar guardado. Las fotos usan IndexedDB. «Restablecer demostración» elimina estos cambios. Pruebas aisladas: ./gradlew test --tests ' FertilizationApiTest'. Navegador: desde frontend, npx playwright test tests/fertilization.spec.ts."}]},{"title":"Water Management","group":"Module guides","page":"water-management.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--water-management","text":"This document describes the implemented water-management workflow in Agro Core. It covers irrigation sectors, soil-moisture monitoring, irrigation scheduling and execution, water usage, water sources, hydraulic infrastructure, water quality, water balances, maintenance, and sensor verification. The module records and compares operational information. It does not calculate an agronomic prescription, open valves automatically, or replace technical review."},{"title":"Requirements coverage","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--requirements-coverage","text":"Requirements coverage ID Requirement Status Current implementation Remaining gap --- --- --- --- --- WM-01 Define irrigation areas with their crop block, area, irrigation system, water source, reference flow, measurement unit, measurement depth, and optional sensor. Implemented Irrigation sectors are persisted and displayed with block, area, system, source, flow, moisture unit, depth, sensor, and technical configuration. Richer hydraulic topology and map layers are still pending. WM-02 Record soil-moisture measurements with date, value, unit, depth, instrument, rainfall, drainage, and notes. Implemented Manual measurements are stored per sector and retain the sector unit and depth. Sensor readings can also be associated with a sector. Reliable production telemetry and broader data-quality workflows remain pending. WM-03 Configure technically justified moisture thresholds and reading freshness. Implemented Each sector supports lower and upper limits, maximum reading age, technical basis, versioning, and revision history. Threshold recommendations and farm/variety-specific agronomic profiles are not automated. WM-04 Schedule irrigation for a sector with a date, time, planned duration, planned volume, responsible person, justification, and instructions. Implemented for one-time and recurring plans Managers can create one-time plans or daily/weekly recurring rules, preview upcoming occurrences, generate idempotent plans/tasks, edit future rules, pause, resume, and cancel them. Approval workflow, calendar integration, and schedule optimization remain pending. WM-05 Record actual irrigation and water consumption. Implemented Execution records capture actual start/end, interruptions, effective duration, volume measured by meter or estimated from flow, notes, and variance reasons. Reports compare planned and actual volume by sector and block, separate measured and estimated consumption, and relate it to rainfall, balance requirements, deficits, and soil-moisture readings. Automated meter reconciliation and weather-service integration remain pending. WM-06 Detect moisture and irrigation exceptions that need review. Implemented for operational alerts The UI flags below-range, above-range, stale, or missing moisture readings, overdue plans, plan deviations, and blocked maintenance. Alert-to-irrigation automation, escalation, and external notification delivery remain pending. WM-07 Manage water sources, quality, and hydraulic assets. Implemented for records and edits Sources support type, capacity, coordinates, notes, active status, and versioned edits. Quality records support pH, conductivity, salinity, suspended solids, laboratory reference, notes, and versioned edits. Assets support pumps, valves, pipes, filters, meters, drains, canals, reservoirs, status, block/source links, coordinates, and versioned edits. Permits and a complete map of hydraulic connections remain pending. WM-08 Track water balance, maintenance, and sensor calibration. Implemented for records and edits Balances store rainfall, ET0, crop coefficient, root depth, available water, required/applied volume, deficit, and versioned edits. Maintenance stores sector or asset, activity, date, status, pressure, flow, notes, completion date, and versioned edits. Sensor checks store calibration status, reference/observed values, technician, notes, and versioned edits. Automated balance calculations, maintenance task integration, and calibration-driven data suppression remain pending."},{"title":"Operational workflow","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--operational-workflow","text":"Operational workflow 1. Configure a sector. A manager associates a sector with a block and records its irrigation system, water source, area, measurement unit, measurement depth, and optional sensor. 2. Set technical limits. The responsible technical user records the lower and upper moisture limits, the maximum age of a valid reading, and the technical basis. Limits are versioned when changed. 3. Measure conditions. A user records a manual reading with its instrument, rainfall, drainage, and notes, or the sector receives a compatible sensor reading. 4. Review conditions. The module classifies the latest reading as below range, in range, above range, stale, or without data when the required information is available. It does not infer an agronomic recommendation from that classification. 5. Schedule irrigation. A manager creates a one-time plan with the target sector, planned date/time, duration, volume, assignee, technical justification, and execution instructions, or creates a recurring rule with a daily/weekly interval and start/end dates. 6. Generate repeated work. Active recurring rules materialize each occurrence as a normal irrigation plan and farm task. The schedule/date uniqueness key and the daily generator prevent duplicate plans or tasks. The background job runs daily (default cron: 00:15 in the API server's time zone) and fills the next 30 days; managers can also preview or generate occurrences and pause, resume, edit, or cancel future generation. Set AGRO IRRIGATION RECURRING CRON through the Spring property agro.irrigation.recurring.cron to change the job schedule. 7. Generate and execute the task. The plan can generate one farm task. The assigned worker or an authorized manager records execution using a meter or a flow estimate. Overlapping executions for the same sector are rejected. 8. Verify and preserve history. Actual volume and duration accumulate against the plan. Exceeding the planned amount requires a reason, and the execution is linked to an IRRIGATION EVENT with optional photo evidence. 9. Correct operational records. Authorized managers can edit water sources, quality analyses, hydraulic assets, balances, maintenance, and sensor checks. Updates are farm-scoped and use optimistic version checks to reject stale edits. 10. Analyze usage. The Reports screen filters water usage by date range and sector/block, presents planned versus applied volume and measured versus estimated origin, compares rainfall and balance deficits, and exports the visible sector report as CSV."},{"title":"Water-management records","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--water-management-records","text":"Water-management records"},{"title":"Soil moisture","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--soil-moisture","text":"Soil moisture The supported units are volumetric percentage (VWC PERCENT), gravimetric percentage (GRAV PERCENT), and kilopascals (KPA). Units and measurement depths must match the sector configuration. Percentage values are constrained to 0–100; the module does not treat the three units as interchangeable. Reading freshness is evaluated per sector using its configured maximum age. A stale or missing reading produces an operational warning rather than an automatic watering decision. Rainfall and drainage are retained as context for later technical review."},{"title":"Irrigation execution and usage","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--irrigation-execution-and-usage","text":"Irrigation execution and usage Meter-based execution stores the meter unit and starting/ending readings. Flow-based execution stores the flow in cubic metres per hour and derives volume from effective duration. Effective duration subtracts recorded interruptions. Multiple partial executions can be recorded against a plan while the plan tracks cumulative actual volume and duration. The module requires a variance reason when cumulative actual volume or duration exceeds the planned values. It rejects future timestamps, invalid meter sequences, non-positive effective duration, and overlapping executions for a sector."},{"title":"Water sources and infrastructure","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--water-sources-and-infrastructure","text":"Water sources and infrastructure Water sources can be wells, reservoirs, canals, rivers, rainwater, or another source. Hydraulic assets can be pumps, valves, pipes, filters, meters, drains, canals, reservoirs, or other assets. Sources and assets may include coordinates and notes; assets can be associated with a block and a water source. Water-quality records are linked to a source and may include laboratory references. The database validates pH from 0 to 14 and prevents negative analytical values."},{"title":"Balances, maintenance, and calibration","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--balances-maintenance-and-calibration","text":"Balances, maintenance, and calibration Water balances are recorded by sector and date. The record can include rainfall, reference evapotranspiration, crop coefficient, root depth, available water, required volume, applied volume, and deficit. A sector may have only one balance for each date. Maintenance can target either a sector or a hydraulic asset, but not both. Supported activities include filter washing, pump and valve checks, leak repair, meter calibration, drain cleaning, and other maintenance. Sensor checks retain the sensor, sector, date, calibration status, reference value, observed value, technician, and notes."},{"title":"User interface","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--user-interface","text":"User interface The Riego screen is organized into four tabs: - Mediciones: sector configuration, moisture history, and operational alerts. - Programación: one-time and recurring schedules, task/occurrence generation, upcoming-occurrence preview, pause/resume/edit/cancel controls, and preserved sector context. - Ejecuciones: actual duration, volume origin, interruptions, variance, and attached evidence; executions can be exported to CSV. - Administración de agua: sources, quality, hydraulic infrastructure, balances, maintenance, and sensor calibration checks. The Reportes screen also includes a water-use report with period and sector/block filters, KPI cards, a sector comparison table, and CSV export. It reads the same farm-scoped irrigation records as the Riego screen and labels demo values as fictitious. The demo mode uses local browser storage and clearly labels its initial data as fictitious. Real mode reads and writes through the server API."},{"title":"API","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--api","text":"API The irrigation controller is rooted at /api/v1/irrigation. Operation Endpoint --- --- List irrigation data GET /api/v1/irrigation?farmId={farmId} Create sector POST /api/v1/irrigation/sectors Update sector thresholds POST /api/v1/irrigation/sectors/{id}/thresholds Record moisture measurement POST /api/v1/irrigation/measurements Create irrigation plan POST /api/v1/irrigation/plans Generate task POST /api/v1/irrigation/plans/{id}/task Cancel plan POST /api/v1/irrigation/plans/{id}/cancel Record execution POST /api/v1/irrigation/plans/{id}/executions Create recurring schedule POST /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules Update recurring schedule PATCH /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules/{id} Generate upcoming recurring plans/tasks POST /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules/{id}/generate Pause recurring schedule POST /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules/{id}/pause Resume recurring schedule POST /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules/{id}/resume Cancel recurring schedule POST /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules/{id}/cancel Preview recurring schedule GET /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules/{id}/preview Read recurring schedule history GET /api/v1/irrigation/recurring-schedules/{id}/history Create water source POST /api/v1/irrigation/water-sources Update water source PATCH /api/v1/irrigation/water-sources/{id} Record water quality POST /api/v1/irrigation/water-sources/{id}/quality Update water quality PATCH /api/v1/irrigation/water-quality/{id} Create hydraulic asset POST /api/v1/irrigation/infrastructure Update hydraulic asset PATCH /api/v1/irrigation/infrastructure/{id} Record water balance POST /api/v1/irrigation/water-balances Update water balance PATCH /api/v1/irrigation/water-balances/{id} Record maintenance POST /api/v1/irrigation/maintenance Update maintenance PATCH /api/v1/irrigation/maintenance/{id} Record sensor check POST /api/v1/irrigation/sensor-checks Update sensor check PATCH /api/v1/irrigation/sensor-checks/{id} The analytics controller is rooted at /api/v1/analytics. Operation Endpoint --- --- Water-use report GET /api/v1/analytics/water-usage?farmId={farmId}&from={yyyy-mm-dd}&until={yyyy-mm-dd} Farm membership and manager permissions are enforced by the backend. Sensor device registration and raw telemetry ingestion are provided by the sensor endpoints and are consumed by irrigation sectors when associated."},{"title":"Persistence and implementation evidence","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--persistence-and-implementation-evidence","text":"Persistence and implementation evidence - src/main/java/com/yourco/agro/irrigation/IrrigationController.java - src/main/java/com/yourco/agro/irrigation/IrrigationService.java - src/main/java/com/yourco/agro/irrigation/IrrigationDtos.java - src/main/resources/db/migration/V11 irrigation workflow.sql - src/main/resources/db/migration/V13 irrigation water and maintenance.sql - src/main/resources/db/migration/V23 irrigation management updates.sql - src/main/resources/db/migration/V17 irrigation recurring schedules.sql - src/main/java/com/yourco/agro/irrigation/IrrigationRecurrenceJob.java - src/main/java/com/yourco/agro/analytics/AnalyticsController.java - src/main/java/com/yourco/agro/analytics/AnalyticsService.java - frontend/src/pages/Irrigation.tsx - frontend/src/pages/Operations.tsx - frontend/src/lib/analytics.ts - frontend/tests/irrigation.spec.ts"},{"title":"Remaining gaps","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--remaining-gaps","text":"Remaining gaps - Full approval, calendar, escalation, and notification workflows. - Automated meter/telemetry reconciliation and weather-service integration. - Complete hydraulic map showing sources, pipes, valves, drains, and relationships. - Permission policies for water-source, infrastructure, and technical-threshold administration. - Offline capture and synchronization for field operations."},{"title":"Verification","anchor":"doc-docs-water-management-md--verification","text":"Verification The focused backend tests are: ./gradlew test --tests com.yourco.agro.IrrigationApiTest ./gradlew test --tests com.yourco.agro.IrrigationPostgresTest The frontend irrigation workflow can be verified from frontend with: npm run build npm run test:e2e -- tests/irrigation.spec.ts -g \"water management records\" npm run test:e2e -- tests/irrigation.spec.ts -g \"recurring irrigation schedules\" npm run test:e2e -- tests/irrigation.spec.ts -g \"water usage report\""}]},{"title":"AgroVision frontend","group":"User interface and operations","page":"frontend.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-frontend-readme-md--agrovision-frontend","text":"Interfaz en español de Agro Core, construida con React, TypeScript y Vite. El diseño toma como referencia las imágenes suministradas: tonos verdes, navegación lateral, tarjetas de información y mapas como centro de la experiencia."},{"title":"Inicio rápido","anchor":"doc-frontend-readme-md--inicio-rápido","text":"Inicio rápido Requiere Node.js 22.18 o superior y npm. cd frontend npm ci npm run dev Abrir http://127.0.0.1:5173. No se necesita el backend para explorar la demostración. Para conectar datos reales, iniciar el API según el README principal y seleccionar Conectar mi finca . Primero se puede registrar una cuenta; después, iniciar sesión y crear una finca o solicitar acceso a su administrador. El proxy de desarrollo envía /api a http://localhost:8080. Para otro destino: AGRO API URL=http://localhost:8081 npm run dev"},{"title":"Pantallas y funciones","anchor":"doc-frontend-readme-md--pantallas-y-funciones","text":"Pantallas y funciones Pantalla Funciones --- --- Dashboard Resumen de área, campos, bloques, plantas estimadas, actividades y acceso al mapa. Indicadores de salud, clima y producción solo en demo. Mi trabajo de hoy Tareas persistentes por campo o bloque, fecha, miembro responsable, prioridad, instrucciones, notas, bloqueo, ejecución, revisión y aprobación. Vista de tarjetas/lista y filtros. Campos y bloques Listas por jerarquía, formularios de creación con coordenadas JSON, filtro por campo y eliminación con confirmación. Surcos y plantas Inventario por bloque, longitud, espaciado, plantas estimadas, creación, eliminación y carga incremental de filas en pantalla. Mapas Bloques seleccionables, acercamiento, centrado, pantalla ampliada, controles de capas y comparación. La capa Nutrientes permite consultar N, P, K, pH, materia orgánica, Ca, Mg, S, B y Zn por bloque, con trazabilidad e interpretación preliminar. En modo real, representa datos del API. Riego Sectores, mediciones de humedad, programación y ejecución de riego, consumo de agua, alertas, fuentes, calidad, infraestructura, balances, mantenimiento y verificación de sensores. Las reglas recurrentes permiten vista previa, generación de ocurrencias idempotente, pausa, reanudación y cancelación; un job diario genera las próximas tareas. Fertilización Pestañas Análisis , Planes y Aplicaciones para registrar resultados de suelo/foliares, interpretar provisionalmente, crear y consultar perfiles/productos, aprobar planes únicos o recurrentes, generar tareas por ocurrencia, registrar aplicaciones parciales, seguimientos y evidencia; sin prescripción automática ni edición/desactivación de perfiles y productos. Plagas y enfermedades Registro de observaciones con síntomas y severidad, historial de eventos y tratamientos. Cosecha Historial estructurado por campo, bloque o fila y ciclo de cultivo; captura de fecha, área, rendimiento estimado, cantidad cosechada, unidad, calidad, rechazo y motivo; filtros por ciclo y unidad; acciones para ver el detalle y editar registros. Reportes y actividad Filtros por tipo, bloque y fechas, detalle de evento, exportación CSV, referencias de archivos y eliminación autorizada. Centro agronómico Orientación de uso y accesos a los registros; sin servicio de IA ni recomendaciones automáticas. Configuración Perfil, información de finca, creación de otra finca, equipo y asignación/cambio de roles. Acceso Registro, login, logout, recuperación de sesión y tratamiento de errores de conexión/permisos. El inventario completo de endpoints y pantallas de nutrición está en docs/fertilization.md. En modo real, el detalle de Análisis usa el perfil activo configurado para la finca; la capa Mapas → Nutrientes todavía usa un perfil provisional incluido en el frontend y puede mostrar otra interpretación. La guía de fertilización explica este límite. La búsqueda superior filtra el contenido de la vista que la admite: registros, tareas, comparaciones por bloque y miembros. No es un buscador global del servidor. Los controles de finca y fecha se muestran en el encabezado de escritorio y sobre el contenido en móvil. La fecha seleccionada se usa para la lista de tareas, con pendientes de días anteriores."},{"title":"Demostración y datos reales","anchor":"doc-frontend-readme-md--demostración-y-datos-reales","text":"Demostración y datos reales Demostración: tiene una franja visible de identificación. Los datos incluyen una finca, tres campos, cinco bloques, 328 surcos y actividades/tareas de ejemplo. Las modificaciones se guardan en localStorage bajo claves agrovision. .v1. El rol se puede simular desde Configuración; no altera permisos del servidor. El flujo de tareas no genera eventos reales ni sincroniza datos. Modo real: usa los endpoints existentes del backend. Los permisos se verifican en el servidor y la UI oculta las acciones de administración a trabajadores. Las tareas, sensores, clima, nutrientes, producción estimada e IA no se sustituyen por valores ficticios en este modo. Los errores del servidor se muestran en pantalla. En Mi trabajo de hoy → Nueva tarea , administradores, supervisores y agrónomos pueden elegir un campo o bloque y asignar un miembro real de la finca. No es necesario crear bloques para asignar tareas a los siete campos de Guasita. Los trabajadores solo consultan y ejecutan sus asignaciones. El flujo es pendiente → en curso → por revisar → completada; se puede reportar un bloqueo con motivo y retomarlo. Administradores, supervisores y agrónomos pueden aprobar o devolver tareas en revisión. Las tareas y notas persisten en PostgreSQL y las actualizaciones simultáneas se rechazan con un aviso para recargar. La fecha se interpreta según el calendario del dispositivo; todavía no hay zona horaria configurada por finca. Completar una tarea no crea un evento agrícola, ni adjunta evidencias. No hay reprogramación, cancelación o edición de asignaciones en esta primera versión. Los mapas de demo utilizan una imagen aérea ficticia y polígonos ilustrativos. No son cartografía georreferenciada. La vista real usa Leaflet para colocar los polígonos SRID 4326 de campos y bloques y las líneas SRID 4326 de los surcos encima de un mapa base. La capa Mapa usa OpenStreetMap; Satélite usa World Imagery de Esri por defecto y Híbrido combina ambas capas. Para producción se puede configurar un proveedor autorizado con VITE MAP SATELLITE TILES URL y VITE MAP SATELLITE ATTRIBUTION. Los campos, bloques y surcos se pueden activar o desactivar desde Herramientas y las geometrías son seleccionables aunque todavía no existan observaciones. Las geometrías marcadas [PRUEBA] se identifican como ficticias en la vista de mapas. Ver el plano de prueba de Guasita. El endpoint de eventos no devuelve los detalles de observación: los síntomas y la severidad se envían al crear el registro, pero el frontend no inventa esos datos al consultarlo. Las referencias S3 anteriores se conservan. La sección Fotos adjuntas del detalle de eventos e inspecciones permite elegir JPEG/PNG desde archivos o cámara, ampliar miniaturas y descargarlas. En inspecciones se pueden asociar a un tratamiento. Se admiten hasta 10 MB y 24 megapíxeles por foto. Las fotos reales se guardan en PostgreSQL con sesión y CSRF; las demo se guardan en IndexedDB, persisten al recargar y se limpian al restablecer la demo. Primero guarda el registro y luego abre su detalle para adjuntar fotos. No se ofrecen botones de edición para recursos cuyo API solo admite creación, consulta y eliminación. La estimación de área utiliza las áreas de campos cuando existen, evitando sumar también las áreas de sus bloques. Las plantas son estimaciones agregadas por surco."},{"title":"Sesión y despliegue","anchor":"doc-frontend-readme-md--sesión-y-despliegue","text":"Sesión y despliegue - api.ts obtiene el token CSRF antes de una escritura y lo renueva después del login. Envía cookies con credentials: include. - Las credenciales y los tokens de autenticación no se guardan en localStorage. sessionStorage conserva únicamente la intención de recuperar una sesión real. - Las fuentes y la imagen aérea de demostración se incluyen localmente mediante Fontsource y public/images. El mapa real sí carga teselas externas: OpenStreetMap para el mapa base y Esri World Imagery para satélite/híbrido por defecto. Revisa atribución y condiciones de uso del proveedor antes de producción; los endpoints VITE MAP SATELLITE TILES URL y VITE MAP SATELLITE ATTRIBUTION permiten configurar las teselas satelitales. - Para producción, ejecutar npm run build y servir dist/ con un proxy /api hacia Spring Boot. AGRO API URL configura el proxy de Vite, no el bundle estático. - Usar HTTPS, cookies seguras y el frontend/API en el mismo sitio. No publicar los valores de base de datos de desarrollo como configuración de producción. - Las tareas locales no ofrecen sincronización, resolución de conflictos ni garantía de persistencia si el navegador elimina sus datos. No es una PWA offline."},{"title":"Verificación","anchor":"doc-frontend-readme-md--verificación","text":"Verificación Para ejecutar las pruebas de backend, frontend y el recorrido automatizado de una finca temporal en Chrome, usa python3 scripts/run qa tests.py desde la raíz. Consulta los requisitos y los informes en la guía de pruebas automatizadas. npm test npm run build npx playwright install chromium npm run test:e2e Las pruebas unitarias verifican separación por finca, métricas, fecha local, selección de tareas, transiciones y escape de CSV. Playwright verifica navegación, mapas, formularios, tareas, permisos de la UI, CSV y el intercambio de login/CSRF con un API simulado, tanto en escritorio como en móvil. Para el recorrido contra un API real, usar exclusivamente una base de verificación aislada: la prueba crea una cuenta, finca, campo, bloque, surco y evento. AGRO LIVE TEST=1 npm run test:e2e -- --project=desktop --grep 'real server onboarding' El API incluye correcciones de integración para serializar geometrías como GeoJSON y evitar propiedades internas de proxies Hibernate, mapear el enum de eventos y los campos JSONB de PostgreSQL, y consultar eventos asociados solo a bloque o solo a surco con filtros opcionales. Las pruebas del backend cubren ese recorrido y el registro de observaciones/mapas/referencias de archivos. También se puede usar Chrome instalado en macOS: PLAYWRIGHT CHROMIUM EXECUTABLE='/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome' npm run test:e2e ```"},{"title":"Organización","anchor":"doc-frontend-readme-md--organización","text":"Organización src/ App.tsx Navegación, sesión, datos y modo demo/real context.ts Estado compartido de la interfaz components/ Mapa, formularios, diálogos y gráficos SVG lib/ Cliente API, tipos, datos demo y reglas probadas pages/ Pantallas de cada módulo styles.css Estilos y adaptación responsive public/images/ Imagen aérea ilustrativa y su especificación tests/ Pruebas de navegador ```"}]},{"title":"Recorrido visible de la finca","group":"User interface and operations","page":"manual-workflow.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--recorrido-visible-de-la-finca","text":"El recorrido se puede ejecutar automáticamente en una finca temporal de QA o de forma manual en una finca de pruebas existente."},{"title":"Pruebas automatizadas en Chrome","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--pruebas-automatizadas-en-chrome","text":"Pruebas automatizadas en Chrome Ejecuta el script de QA desde la raíz del repositorio. python3 scripts/run qa tests.py Necesitas Python 3, JDK 17, Node.js 22.18 o posterior, Docker en ejecución y Google Chrome instalado. Instala las dependencias del frontend con npm ci desde frontend antes de la primera ejecución. En macOS el script busca el JDK 17 instalado si JAVA HOME apunta a otra versión. 1. El script inicia PostGIS en un contenedor temporal y usa esa base para las pruebas del backend. 2. Ejecuta las pruebas unitarias y la compilación del frontend. 3. Levanta una API local y abre Chrome para el recorrido de la finca. Registra una cuenta temporal, inicia sesión, crea la finca QA y recorre los flujos de campo, bloque, surco, riego, fertilización y cosecha. Una tarjeta indica cada paso y el recorrido avanza automáticamente. 4. Ejecuta la suite de Playwright en ventanas visibles de Chrome para escritorio y móvil. Incluye el alta real de una cuenta y sus primeros registros agrícolas contra la API de QA. Para leer con más calma cada paso, puedes aumentar la pausa. La pausa predeterminada es de un segundo. python3 scripts/run qa tests.py --step-delay-ms 2000 El script usa postgis/postgis:16-3.7 por defecto. Puedes seleccionar otra imagen de PostGIS con --postgis-image. En la primera ejecución se pueden descargar la imagen y las dependencias de Gradle. La API y PostgreSQL usan puertos locales separados de los servicios de desarrollo. El script detiene la API y elimina el contenedor y sus datos de QA al finalizar, incluso si falla una prueba. Los informes y las capturas quedan disponibles para revisar el resultado. Resultado Ubicación --- --- Pruebas de Gradle build/reports/tests/test/index.html Informes de Playwright frontend/playwright-report/<id>/browser/ y frontend/playwright-report/<id>/walkthrough/ Capturas y log de la API frontend/test-results/<id>/ La terminal muestra el identificador de la ejecución y el resultado de cada etapa. El comando devuelve un código distinto de cero si alguna etapa falla. Para revisar los pasos y las capturas del recorrido, ejecuta desde frontend el comando siguiente con ese identificador. npx playwright show-report \"playwright-report/ID DE EJECUCION/walkthrough\" ```"},{"title":"Última ejecución completa verificada","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--última-ejecución-completa-verificada","text":"Última ejecución completa verificada El 25 de noviembre de 2026, la ejecución agro-qa-20261125-102610-d61d9aec completó todas las etapas con Chrome visible para las pruebas de navegador. Verificación Resultado Informe --- --- --- Playwright en Chrome, escritorio y móvil 58 aprobadas, 0 fallidas, 0 omitidas Informe completo de navegador Recorrido automatizado de la finca QA 1 aprobado, desde el inicio de sesión hasta el registro de cosecha Informe del recorrido QA Backend con Gradle y PostGIS 47 aprobadas, 0 fallidas, 0 omitidas Informe de Gradle Pruebas unitarias del frontend 11 aprobadas, 0 fallidas Salida de npm test en la terminal Compilación del frontend Completada Salida de npm run build en la terminal Los enlaces apuntan a archivos generados localmente. Cada ejecución guarda sus informes de Playwright con un identificador propio. Gradle actualiza su informe en la misma ruta al volver a ejecutar las pruebas."},{"title":"Correcciones verificadas","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--correcciones-verificadas","text":"Correcciones verificadas - Campos en móvil. El botón Nuevo campo vuelve a estar disponible. La barra de acciones se ajusta a pantallas estrechas. - Mapas. La brújula deja libre el control de zoom y el panel lateral de escritorio queda debajo de la barra de capas. Las pruebas comprueban el zoom, la comparación de humedad y nutrientes en un solo mapa y la selección de bloques tras cerrar los paneles superpuestos. - Riego e inspecciones. Las pruebas usan las entradas actuales de Sectores e Infraestructura , verifican el volumen estimado y la duración efectiva en la tabla de ejecuciones, y comprueban que la revisión de una inspección cambia el estado que muestra el mapa sanitario. - Fotos y CSV. La prueba de fotos usa una respuesta simulada con el formato del informe de consumo de agua y comprueba la carga autenticada y la vista previa tras recargar. La prueba de exportación verifica la tabla de actividades y que el CSV descargado solo contiene la actividad filtrada."},{"title":"Recorrido con inicio de sesión manual","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--recorrido-con-inicio-de-sesión-manual","text":"Recorrido con inicio de sesión manual La prueba manual-agricultural-workflow.spec.ts automatiza el recorrido de una finca existente después de que tú inicies sesión manualmente. Crea datos reales marcados como prueba, por lo que se debe usar contra una finca aislada de QA o una copia de la finca, nunca contra información operativa."},{"title":"Qué verifica","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--qué-verifica","text":"Qué verifica 1. Inicio de sesión manual en una ventana de Chrome visible. 2. Selección explícita de la finca indicada. 3. Campo, lote/bloque y surco. 4. Sector, medición, programación, tarea y ejecución de riego. 5. Análisis, plan, aprobación, tarea, aplicación y seguimiento de fertilización. 6. Ciclo de cultivo y registro estructurado de cosecha. La aplicación actual no ofrece un plan de cosecha separado. El último paso comprueba el flujo que sí existe: ciclo de cultivo + registro de cosecha con rendimiento, calidad y merma."},{"title":"Requisitos","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--requisitos","text":"Requisitos - API de Agro Core levantada en http://localhost:8080. - Frontend con dependencias instaladas: cd frontend && npm ci. - Chromium disponible para Playwright: npx playwright install chromium. - Una sesión ADMIN o SUPERVISOR de la finca. Este recorrido crea y aprueba planes de fertilización, cuyas acciones de gestión requieren uno de esos dos roles. AGRONOMIST puede configurar perfiles y revisar análisis, pero no puede crear ni aprobar esos planes por sí solo. - Al menos un cultivo creado en el catálogo de esa finca; se necesita para crear el bloque y el ciclo. - Una finca de pruebas. La prueba no borra sus registros para que puedas inspeccionar cada resultado."},{"title":"Ejecución paso a paso","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--ejecución-paso-a-paso","text":"Ejecución paso a paso Desde la carpeta frontend, reemplaza el nombre de la finca por el nombre exacto que aparece en el selector de la aplicación: AGRO MANUAL E2E=1 \\ AGRO E2E ALLOW WRITES=SI \\ AGRO E2E FARM NAME='Finca QA' \\ PWDEBUG=1 \\ npx playwright test tests/manual-agricultural-workflow.spec.ts --project=desktop --workers=1 Se abrirán Chrome y Playwright Inspector. Inicia sesión tú mismo; el script se queda detenido. Después pulsa Resume en el Inspector. Tras cada etapa el script vuelve a detenerse, deja una tarjeta verde en la esquina inferior derecha de la página y te permite revisar exactamente qué creó antes de continuar. Para ejecutar el mismo recorrido visible sin detenerlo en cada etapa: AGRO MANUAL E2E=1 \\ AGRO E2E ALLOW WRITES=SI \\ AGRO E2E FARM NAME='Finca QA' \\ AGRO E2E PAUSE EVERY STEP=0 \\ AGRO E2E STEP DELAY MS=1200 \\ npx playwright test tests/manual-agricultural-workflow.spec.ts --project=desktop --workers=1 --headed"},{"title":"Seguridad y personalización","anchor":"doc-frontend-tests-manual-agricultural-workflow-md--seguridad-y-personalización","text":"Seguridad y personalización - AGRO E2E ALLOW WRITES=SI es obligatorio para evitar ejecuciones accidentales. - AGRO E2E FARM NAME es obligatorio: la prueba no elige la primera finca por defecto. - Cada registro lleva E2E-MANUAL y una marca de fecha/hora. Puedes cambiar el prefijo con AGRO E2E PREFIX='QA-REGRESION'. - Por defecto las geometrías se crean alrededor de -74.1, 10.5. Para ubicarlas en un punto de prueba de tu mapa, define AGRO E2E LONGITUDE y AGRO E2E LATITUDE. - Al finalizar, Playwright guarda video, trace, capturas y una captura final en frontend/test-results/. Puedes abrir un trace con npx playwright show-trace test-results/<carpeta>/trace.zip."}]},{"title":"Plano de prueba de Guasita","group":"User interface and operations","page":"map-fixture.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-scripts-readme-md--plano-de-prueba-de-guasita","text":"guasita-test-map.sql crea siete campos en la finca existente Guasita: cuatro de 10 ha y tres de 14, 13 y 13 ha, para un total nominal de 80 ha. Todos se identifican con [PRUEBA]. No se crean cultivos, bloques, surcos ni observaciones. El centro aproximado es longitud -74.82109, latitud 10.02539, como referencia de Tasajera, El Guamo, Bolívar. La posición de Guasita, su perímetro y sus divisiones son ficticios; requieren coordenadas reales para representar la finca. El plano no es un levantamiento topográfico. Los rectángulos se construyen en UTM 18N y se guardan en WGS84/SRID 4326. La escala se ajusta con el área geodésica del conjunto para aproximar 80 ha. Desde la raíz del proyecto, con PostgreSQL iniciado: docker compose exec -T db psql -U agro -d agro < scripts/guasita-test-map.sql ``` El script solo trabaja si hay exactamente una finca llamada Guasita y no tiene campos. Si ya existen los siete campos de prueba, no los duplica. Si hay otros campos, se detiene. No elimina ni reemplaza datos existentes. Después, recargar el frontend y abrir Mapas . El plano dibuja las geometrías de campos y bloques almacenadas en el API, aunque no tengan observaciones. Los campos se pueden seleccionar para ver su área. El script solo crea geometrías ficticias: no incluye imágenes aéreas ni cartografía propia. La vista real muestra OpenStreetMap como mapa base y ofrece capas satelital e híbrida de Esri; esas teselas son independientes de los datos de prueba. Las geometrías pueden sustituirse más adelante mediante un flujo de edición; el API actual solo permite crear, consultar y eliminar campos."}]},{"title":"Imagen aérea de demostración","group":"User interface and operations","page":"aerial-image.html","sections":[{"title":"Overview","anchor":"doc-frontend-public-images-readme-md--imagen-aérea-de-demostración","text":"Archivo: farm-aerial.png. Generado con la herramienta integrada imagegen para este proyecto. Es una imagen ficticia; no representa la finca de una persona ni una ubicación verificada. Se utiliza como fondo ilustrativo y no como cartografía real. Los controles, bloques, capas y etiquetas se dibujan por separado mediante código SVG. Prompt utilizado: > Use case: photorealistic-natural. Asset type: background raster for an agricultural dashboard interactive map. Create a realistic, beautiful strictly top-down drone aerial photograph of a tropical Colombian banana plantation, landscape 3:2 composition. A large agricultural estate with five clear adjacent irregular rectangular green banana plantation blocks occupying most of the image, many precise parallel rows of banana trees, separated by narrow beige dirt tracks. Dense lush tropical forest surrounds the estate at all edges, a winding blue-green river along the far right edge, a tiny simple farm shed in the lower right. Rich natural forest greens, sunlit lime banana leaves, subtle warm tropical sunlight, high-resolution fine canopy texture, professional satellite agricultural imagery, natural photography. No text, no labels, no borders, no UI, no colored heatmaps, no diagrams, no watermarks. This will be a fictional illustrative demo basemap, not real geospatial data."}]}];
